\\frac{2x+7}{|x|+{x}^{2}+1}\\ge \\frac{x+4}{|x|+{x}^{2}+1}.$\n\n$x$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}" "Solve the inequality\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{2x+7}{|x|+{x}^{2}+1}\\ge \\frac{x+4}{|x|+{x}^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\n$x$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}"] [:db/cas 17592186304796 :question/problem "Find the parametric equations of the line $\\frac{x-2}{2}=\\frac{y-1}{4}=\\frac{z+1}{3}.$" "Find the parametric equations of the line $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{x-2}{2}=\\frac{y-1}{4}=\\frac{z+1}{3}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186304803 :question/problem "Consider the straight line with the canonical equation $\\frac{x-3}{5}=\\frac{y-1}{1}=\\frac{z+7}{-5}.$ Find the vector equation of the line." "Consider the straight line with the canonical equation $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{x-3}{5}=\\frac{y-1}{1}=\\frac{z+7}{-5}.\n$$ Find the vector equation of the line."] [:db/cas 17592186304822 :question/worked-solution "We divide both sides of the equation by $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and use the identity $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =8\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =\\frac{8\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\ \\frac{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =\\frac{8\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}} \\\\ 3\\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =8 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =\\frac{8}{3} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =\\frac{8}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{8}{3}\\right)≈1.212\\,03\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $5$ decimal places. \n\nTo find the other solution in the given interval, we add $\\pi $ to the principal value. Hence,\n\n$$\nx≈1.212\\,03+\\pi ≈4.353\\,62\n$$\n\nrounded to $5$ decimal places. \n\nSo, in ascending order, rounded to two decimal places, the solutions are \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}≈1.21,\\,{x}_{2}≈4.35.\n$$" "We divide both sides of the equation by $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and use the identity $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.\n$$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =8\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =\\frac{8\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\ \\frac{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =\\frac{8\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}} \\\\ 3\\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =8 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =\\frac{8}{3} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =\\frac{8}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{8}{3}\\right)≈1.212\\,03\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $5$ decimal places. \n\nTo find the other solution in the given interval, we add $\\pi $ to the principal value. Hence,\n\n$$\nx≈1.212\\,03+\\pi ≈4.353\\,62\n$$\n\nrounded to $5$ decimal places. \n\nSo, in ascending order, rounded to two decimal places, the solutions are \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}≈1.21,\\,{x}_{2}≈4.35.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186304825 :question/worked-solution "We divide both sides of the equation by $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and use the identity $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =\\frac{5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\ 2\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =5 \\\\ 2\\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =5 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right)≈1.190\\,29\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $5$ decimal places. \n\nTo find another solution in the interval $[-\\pi ,\\pi )$, we subtract $\\pi :$\n\n$$\nx≈1.190\\,29-\\pi ≈-1.951\\,30\n$$\n\nrounded to $5$ decimal places. \n\nSo, in ascending order, rounded to two decimal places, the solutions are \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}≈-1.95,\\,{x}_{2}≈1.19.\n$$" "We divide both sides of the equation by $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and use the identity $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =\\frac{5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\ 2\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =5 \\\\ 2\\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =5 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right)≈1.190\\,29\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $5$ decimal places. \n\nTo find another solution in the interval $[-\\pi ,\\pi )$, we subtract $\\pi :$\n\n$$\nx≈1.190\\,29-\\pi ≈-1.951\\,30\n$$\n\nrounded to $5$ decimal places. \n\nSo, in ascending order, rounded to two decimal places, the solutions are \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}≈-1.95,\\,{x}_{2}≈1.19.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186304828 :question/worked-solution "We divide each term of the equation by $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and use the identity $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-7\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}-\\frac{7\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}-\\frac{7\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}} & =0 \\\\ 2\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-7 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =7 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =\\frac{7}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{7}{2}\\right)≈1.292\\,50\n$$\n\nrounded to $5$ decimal places.\n\nTo find the other solution in the given interval, we add $\\pi $ to the principal value. Hence,\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}≈1.292\\,50+\\pi ≈4.434\\,09\n$$\n\nrounded to $5$ decimal places.\n\nSo, in ascending order, rounded to two decimal places, the solutions are \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}≈1.29,\\,{x}_{2}≈4.43.\n$$" "We divide each term of the equation by $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and use the identity $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.\n$$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-7\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}-\\frac{7\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}-\\frac{7\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}} & =0 \\\\ 2\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-7 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =7 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =\\frac{7}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{7}{2}\\right)≈1.292\\,50\n$$\n\nrounded to $5$ decimal places.\n\nTo find the other solution in the given interval, we add $\\pi $ to the principal value. Hence,\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}≈1.292\\,50+\\pi ≈4.434\\,09\n$$\n\nrounded to $5$ decimal places.\n\nSo, in ascending order, rounded to two decimal places, the solutions are \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}≈1.29,\\,{x}_{2}≈4.43.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186304840 :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation to isolate $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+7 & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+7 \\\\ \\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can't find an equivalent equation involving tangent, since this would lead to us dividing by $0:$\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{1}{0}=\\text{undefined}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can instead find an equivalent equation using the fact that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}=0\n$$\n\nIn this case, we can find the principal solution as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ x & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value lies inside the desired domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nTo find more solutions, we add integer multiples of $\\pi $ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}+\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, our solutions are $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$" "First, we rearrange the equation to isolate $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+7 & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+7 \\\\ \\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can't find an equivalent equation involving tangent, since this would lead to us dividing by $0:$\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{1}{0}=\\text{undefined}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can instead find an equivalent equation using the fact that $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}:\n$$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}=0\n$$\n\nIn this case, we can find the principal solution as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ x & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value lies inside the desired domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nTo find more solutions, we add 