n$$" "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c\n$$\n\nthe increment in $y$ using the Runge-Kutta (RK4) method with step size $Δx$ is computed as the weighted average of four sample slopes:\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{6}({y}^{′}+2{y}_{1}^{′}+2{y}_{2}^{′}+{y}_{3}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}^{′}=f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the initial point $(x,y),$\n\n- ${y}_{1}^{′}=f({x}_{1},{y}_{1})$ is the estimated slope at $$\n\\displaystyle ({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{′}\\cdot Δx),\n$$\n\n- ${y}_{2}^{′}=f({x}_{2},{y}_{2})$ is the estimated slope at $$\n\\displaystyle ({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{1}^{′}\\cdot Δx),\n$$\n\n- ${y}_{3}^{′}=f({x}_{3},{y}_{3})$ is the estimated slope at $({x}_{3},{y}_{3})=(x+Δx,\\,y+{y}_{2}^{′}\\cdot Δx).$\n\nAll four sample slopes are already computed. So, we just need to find the increment on $y.$\n\nWe compute $Δy$ using the Runge-Kutta method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{6}({y}^{′}+2{y}_{1}^{′}+2{y}_{2}^{′}+{y}_{3}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(2+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{5}{4}+\\left(-\\frac{1}{4}\\right))\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\cdot \\frac{21}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed first row is as given below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $-1$ | $2$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{5}{4}$ | $-\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{7}{16}$ |\n\nTo get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x$ and $Δy$ to $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx \\\\  & =-1+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =-1+\\frac{7}{16} \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these rows to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $-1$ | $2$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{5}{4}$ | $-\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{7}{16}$ |\n| $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $-\\frac{9}{16}$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y\\,\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)≈-\\frac{9}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186304928 :question/problem "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(-3,0,4)$ and is perpendicular to the line \n$r=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$" "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(-3,0,4)$ and is perpendicular to the line \n$$\n\\displaystyle r=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186304944 :question/worked-solution "Starting from the definition of the Laplace transform, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{e}^{-3t}g(t)} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\cdot {e}^{-3t}\\,g(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-(s+3)t}\\,g(t)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $G(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\,g(t)\\,\\text{d}t,$ where $s>{s}_{0}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-(s+3)t}\\,g(t)\\,\\text{d}t=G(s+3),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\ns+3>{s}_{0}\\,⇒\\,s>{s}_{0}-3.\n$$" "Starting from the definition of the Laplace transform, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{e}^{-3t}g(t)} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\cdot {e}^{-3t}\\,g(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-(s+3)t}\\,g(t)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $$\n\\displaystyle G(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\,g(t)\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$ where $s>{s}_{0}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-(s+3)t}\\,g(t)\\,\\text{d}t=G(s+3),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\ns+3>{s}_{0}\\,⇒\\,s>{s}_{0}-3.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186304945 :question/worked-solution "Starting from the definition of the Laplace transform, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{e}^{-8t}m(t)} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\cdot {e}^{-8t}\\,m(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-(s+8)t}\\,m(t)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $M(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\,m(t)\\,\\text{d}t,$ where $s>{s}_{0}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-(s+8)t}\\,m(t)\\,\\text{d}t=M(s+8),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\ns+8>{s}_{0}\\,⇒\\,s>{s}_{0}-8.\n$$" "Starting from the definition of the Laplace transform, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{e}^{-8t}m(t)} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\cdot {e}^{-8t}\\,m(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-(s+8)t}\\,m(t)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $$\n\\displaystyle M(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\,m(t)\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$ where $s>{s}_{0}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-(s+8)t}\\,m(t)\\,\\text{d}t=M(s+8),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\ns+8>{s}_{0}\\,⇒\\,s>{s}_{0}-8.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186304946 :question/worked-solution "Starting from the definition of the Laplace transform, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{e}^{-9t}d(t)\\} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\cdot {e}^{-9t}\\,d(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-(s+9)t}\\,d(t)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $D(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\,d(t)\\,\\text{d}t,$ where $s>{s}_{0}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-(s+9)t}\\,d(t)\\,\\text{d}t=D(s+9),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\ns+9>{s}_{0}\\,⇒\\,s>{s}_{0}-9.\n$$" "Starting from the definition of the Laplace transform, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{e}^{-9t}d(t)\\} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\cdot {e}^{-9t}\\,d(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-(s+9)t}\\,d(t)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $$\n\\displaystyle D(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\,d(t)\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$ where $s>{s}_{0}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-(s+9)t}\\,d(t)\\,\\text{d}t=D(s+9),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\ns+9>{s}_{0}\\,⇒\\,s>{s}_{0}-9.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186304948 :question/problem "Given that $L{t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t)}=\\frac{2\\omega s}{({s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2})^{2}},$ find the Laplace transform $L{{e}^{3t}\\cdot t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)}.$" "Given that $$\n\\displaystyle L{t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t)}=\\frac{2\\omega s}{({s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2})^{2}},\n$$ find the Laplace transform $L{{e}^{3t}\\cdot t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186304949 :question/problem "Given that $L{t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t)}=\\frac{{s}^{2}-{\\omega }^{2}}{({s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2})^{2}},$ find the Laplace transform $L{{e}^{3t}\\cdot t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)}.$" "Given that $$\n\\displaystyle L{t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t)}=\\frac{{s}^{2}-{\\omega }^{2}}{({s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2})^{2}},\n$$ find the Laplace transform $L{{e}^{3t}\\cdot t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186304950 :question/problem "Given that  $L\\{t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t)\\}=\\frac{{s}^{2}-{\\omega }^{2}}{({s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2})^{2}},$ find the Laplace transform $L\\{{e}^{4t}\\cdot t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\}.$" "Given that  $$\n\\displaystyle L\\{t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t)\\}=\\frac{{s}^{2}-{\\omega }^{2}}{({s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2})^{2}},\n$$ find the Laplace transform $L\\{{e}^{4t}\\cdot t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186304968 :question/worked-solution "A *decoupled system* consists of equations depending on only one variable, such as\n\n$$\n{x}_{i}^{′}(t)={\\lambda }_{i}{x}_{i}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\lambda }_{i}$ is a constant. This means that we can solve each equation independently. \n\nWith this in mind, let's examine our systems.\n\n- System $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=3{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=-{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.$ is *not* decoupled because some of its equations depend on more than one variable. For example, ${x}_{1}^{′}(t)=3{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t)$ depends on ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\n- System $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=4{x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &={x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.$ is *not* decoupled because some of its equations depend on more than one variable. For example, ${x}_{2}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t)$ depends on ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\n- System $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=-9{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=6{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.$ is decoupled since its equations depend on only one variable each. Indeed, ${x}_{1}^{′}(t)=-9{x}_{1}(t)$ depends on only ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}^{′}(t)=6{x}_{2}(t)$ depends on only ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=-9{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=6{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.$ only." "A *decoupled system* consists of equations depending on only one variable, such as\n\n$$\n{x}_{i}^{′}(t)={\\lambda }_{i}{x}_{i}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\lambda }_{i}$ is a constant. This means that we can solve each equation independently. \n\nWith this in mind, let's examine our systems.\n\n- System $$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=3{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=-{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$ is *not* decoupled because some of its equations depend on more than one variable. For example, ${x}_{1}^{′}(t)=3{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t)$ depends on ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\n- System $$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=4{x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &={x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$ is *not* decoupled because some of its equations depend on more than one variable. For example, ${x}_{2}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t)$ depends on ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\n- System $$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=-9{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=6{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$ is decoupled since its equations depend on only one variable each. Indeed, ${x}_{1}^{′}(t)=-9{x}_{1}(t)$ depends on only ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}^{′}(t)=6{x}_{2}(t)$ depends on only ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=-9{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=6{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186304969 :question/worked-solution "A *decoupled system* consists of equations depending on only one variable, such as\n\n$$\n{x}_{i}^{′}(t)={\\lambda }_{i}{x}_{i}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\lambda }_{i}$ is a constant. This means that we can solve each equation independently. \n\nWith this in mind, let's examine our systems.\n\n- System $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=-12{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=5{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.$ is decoupled since its equations depend on only one variable each. Indeed, ${x}_{1}^{′}(t)=-12{x}_{1}(t)$ depends on only ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}^{′}(t)=5{x}_{2}(t)$ depends on only ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\n- System $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=2{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=5{x}_{1}(t)-3{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.$ is *not* decoupled because some of its equations depend on more than one variable. For example, ${x}_{1}^{′}(t)=2{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t)$ depends on ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\n- System $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &={x}_{1}(t)+6{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &={x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.$ is *not* decoupled because some of its equations depend on more than one variable. For example, ${x}_{1}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)+6{x}_{2}(t)$ depends on ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=-12{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=5{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.$ only." "A *decoupled system* consists of equations depending on only one variable, such as\n\n$$\n{x}_{i}^{′}(t)={\\lambda }_{i}{x}_{i}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\lambda }_{i}$ is a constant. This means that we can solve each equation independently. \n\nWith this in mind, let's examine our systems.\n\n- System $$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=-12{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=5{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$ is decoupled since its equations depend on only one variable each. Indeed, ${x}_{1}^{′}(t)=-12{x}_{1}(t)$ depends on only ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}^{′}(t)=5{x}_{2}(t)$ depends on only ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\n- System $$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=2{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=5{x}_{1}(t)-3{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$ is *not* decoupled because some of its equations depend on more than one variable. For example, ${x}_{1}^{′}(t)=2{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t)$ depends on ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\n- System $$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &={x}_{1}(t)+6{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &={x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$ is *not* decoupled because some of its equations depend on more than one variable. For example, ${x}_{1}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)+6{x}_{2}(t)$ depends on ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=-12{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=5{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186304981 :question/problem "Let $f(t)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.$ Find ${h}^{′}(\\pi )$ given the function $h$ is defined by $h(x)={\\int }_{x}^{2\\pi }f(t)\\,\\text{d}t$ for $0<x<2\\pi .$\n\n${h}^{′}(\\pi )=$\n{{field-1}}" "Let $f(t)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.$ Find ${h}^{′}(\\pi )$ given the function $h$ is defined by $$\n\\displaystyle h(x)={\\int }_{x}^{2\\pi }f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$ for $0<x<2\\pi .$\n\n${h}^{′}(\\pi )=$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186304982 :question/problem "Let $f(t)=2{t}^{3}+{t}^{2}-1.$ Find ${h}^{′}(0)$ given the function $h$ is defined by $h(x)={\\int }_{x}^{1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t$ for $x<1.$\n\n${h}^{′}(0)=$\n{{field-1}}" "Let $f(t)=2{t}^{3}+{t}^{2}-1.$ Find ${h}^{′}(0)$ given the function $h$ is defined by $$\n\\displaystyle h(x)={\\int }_{x}^{1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$ for $x<1.$\n\n${h}^{′}(0)=$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186304985 :question/problem "Find the **exact** value of the expression below.\n\n${cos}^{2}\\,\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{8}\\right)-{sin}^{2}\\,\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{8}\\right)=$\n{{field-1}}" "Find the **exact** value of the expression below.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {cos}^{2}\\,\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{8}\\right)-{sin}^{2}\\,\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{8}\\right)=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186304986 :question/problem "Find the **exact** value of the expression below.\n\n${cos}^{2}\\,\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{12}\\right)-{sin}^{2}\\,\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{12}\\right)=$\n{{field-1}}" "Find the **exact** value of the expression below.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {cos}^{2}\\,\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{12}\\right)-{sin}^{2}\\,\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{12}\\right)=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186304989 :question/problem "Find the **exact** value of the expression below.\n\n${cos}^{4}\\,\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{12}\\right)-2{cos}^{2}\\,\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{12}\\right){sin}^{2}\\,\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{12}\\right)+{sin}^{4}\\,\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{12}\\right)=$\n{{field-1}}" "Find the **exact** value of the expression below.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {cos}^{4}\\,\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{12}\\right)-2{cos}^{2}\\,\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{12}\\right){sin}^{2}\\,\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{12}\\right)+{sin}^{4}\\,\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{12}\\right)=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186304990 :question/problem "Find the **exact** value of the expression below.\n\n${cos}^{4}\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)-2{cos}^{2}\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right){sin}^{2}\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)+{sin}^{4}\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)=$\n{{field-1}}" "Find the **exact** value of the expression below.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {cos}^{4}\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)-2{cos}^{2}\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right){sin}^{2}\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)+{sin}^{4}\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186305052 :question/problem "Find the general solution to the system of differential equations\n$\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=2{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=-5{x}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\\right.$" "Find the general solution to the system of differential equations\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=2{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=-5{x}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186305064 :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=2$ with the corresponding eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=5$ with the corresponding eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{2t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{5t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{2t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on the $x$-axis. Since ${e}^{2t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{5t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on the $y$-axis. Since ${e}^{5t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nAmong the given options, the only phase portrait satisfying these conditions is the following:\n\n![](images/89/897778556ebc80f44cf48e17c4fc085c08c45b93358dec9fb0c958de3105c98d.png)" "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=2$ with the corresponding eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=5$ with the corresponding eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{2t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{5t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{1}{e}^{2t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nlie on the $x$-axis. Since ${e}^{2t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{2}{e}^{5t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nlie on the $y$-axis. Since ${e}^{5t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nAmong the given options, the only phase portrait satisfying these conditions is the following:\n\n![](images/89/897778556ebc80f44cf48e17c4fc085c08c45b93358dec9fb0c958de3105c98d.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186305073 :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $8\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$ So, we assume the particular solution to be \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡x,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+4(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡x)=8\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n(4\\alpha +\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+(-\\alpha +4\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=8\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4\\alpha +\\beta &=0\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\ -\\alpha +4\\beta &=8\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{8}{17},\\beta =\\frac{32}{17}.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-\\frac{8}{17}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+\\frac{32}{17}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x).\n$$" "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $8\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$ So, we assume the particular solution to be \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡x,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+4(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡x)=8\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n(4\\alpha +\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+(-\\alpha +4\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=8\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4\\alpha +\\beta &=0\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\ -\\alpha +4\\beta &=8\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $$\n\\displaystyle \\alpha =-\\frac{8}{17},\\beta =\\frac{32}{17}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-\\frac{8}{17}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+\\frac{32}{17}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186305091 :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $8\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ So, we assume the particular solution to be \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡x,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+3(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡x)=8\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n(3\\alpha +\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+(-\\alpha +3\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=8\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3\\alpha +\\beta &=8\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\ -\\alpha +3\\beta &=0\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =\\frac{12}{5},\\beta =\\frac{4}{5}.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\frac{12}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x).\n$$" "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $8\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ So, we assume the particular solution to be \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡x,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+3(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡x)=8\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n(3\\alpha +\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+(-\\alpha +3\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=8\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3\\alpha +\\beta &=8\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\ -\\alpha +3\\beta &=0\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $$\n\\displaystyle \\alpha =\\frac{12}{5},\\beta =\\frac{4}{5}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\frac{12}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186305092 :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $10\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ So, we assume the particular solution to be \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡x,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+5(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡x)=10\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n(5\\alpha +\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+(-\\alpha +5\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=10\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}5\\alpha +\\beta &=10\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\ -\\alpha +5\\beta &=0\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =\\frac{25}{13},\\beta =\\frac{5}{13}.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\frac{25}{13}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+\\frac{5}{13}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x).\n$$" "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $10\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ So, we assume the particular solution to be \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡x,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+5(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡x)=10\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n(5\\alpha +\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+(-\\alpha +5\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=10\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}5\\alpha +\\beta &=10\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\ -\\alpha +5\\beta &=0\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $$\n\\displaystyle \\alpha =\\frac{25}{13},\\beta =\\frac{5}{13}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\frac{25}{13}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+\\frac{5}{13}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186305093 :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{bmatrix}x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ with the corresponding eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=-5$ with the corresponding eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{3t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{-5t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{3t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on the $x$-axis. Since ${e}^{3t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{-5t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on the $y$-axis. Since ${e}^{-5t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nAmong the given options, the only phase portrait satisfying these conditions is the following:\n\n\\\n\n![](images/3f/3facfdf59a92a9202d834cd77d14e009c4ec69c5f1b7bd5f12bd17ea1bbc338b.png)" "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{bmatrix}x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ with the corresponding eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=-5$ with the corresponding eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{3t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{-5t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{1}{e}^{3t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nlie on the $x$-axis. Since ${e}^{3t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{2}{e}^{-5t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nlie on the $y$-axis. Since ${e}^{-5t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nAmong the given options, the only phase portrait satisfying these conditions is the following:\n\n\\\n\n![](images/3f/3facfdf59a92a9202d834cd77d14e009c4ec69c5f1b7bd5f12bd17ea1bbc338b.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186305096 :question/worked-solution "Writing a decoupled system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2}\\end{bmatrix}x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are parallel to the axes. These correspond to so-called straight-line solutions of the form\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\text{and}\\,{c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The tangents along the $x$-axis are pointing away from the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nmust approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{1}>0.$\n\n- The tangents along the $y$-axis are pointing toward the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nmust approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{2}<0.$\n\nAmong the options, the only system that satisfies ${\\lambda }_{1}>0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}<0$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=4x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=-y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" "Writing a decoupled system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2}\\end{bmatrix}x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are parallel to the axes. These correspond to so-called straight-line solutions of the form\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\text{and}\\,{c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The tangents along the $x$-axis are pointing away from the origin, meaning that the solutions of th