}t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The tangents along the $x$-axis are pointing toward the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nmust approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{1}<0.$\n\n- The tangents along the $y$-axis are also pointing toward the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nmust also approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{2}<0.$\n\nAmong the options, the only system that satisfies ${\\lambda }_{1}<0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}<0$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=-6x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=-3y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" "Writing a decoupled system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2}\\end{bmatrix}x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are parallel to the axes. These correspond to so-called straight-line solutions of the form\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\text{and}\\,{c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The tangents along the $x$-axis are pointing toward the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form \n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nmust approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{1}<0.$\n\n- The tangents along the $y$-axis are also pointing toward the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form \n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nmust also approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{2}<0.$\n\nAmong the options, the only system that satisfies ${\\lambda }_{1}<0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}<0$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=-6x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=-3y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186305115 :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n6\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}-12{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{2x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $9\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x).$ So, we assume the particular solution to be \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n6(-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))-12(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x))=9\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x).\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n(18\\beta -12\\alpha )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+(-18\\alpha -12\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)=9\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x).\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}18\\beta -12\\alpha &=9\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)) \\\\ -18\\alpha -12\\beta &=0\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x))\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{3}{13},\\beta =\\frac{9}{26}.$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-\\frac{3}{13}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+\\frac{9}{26}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{2x}+\\left(-\\frac{3}{13}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+\\left(\\frac{9}{26}\\right)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).\n$$" "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n6\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}-12{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{2x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $9\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x).$ So, we assume the particular solution to be \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n6(-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))-12(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x))=9\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x).\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n(18\\beta -12\\alpha )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+(-18\\alpha -12\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)=9\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x).\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}18\\beta -12\\alpha &=9\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)) \\\\ -18\\alpha -12\\beta &=0\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x))\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $$\n\\displaystyle \\alpha =-\\frac{3}{13},\\beta =\\frac{9}{26}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-\\frac{3}{13}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+\\frac{9}{26}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{2x}+\\left(-\\frac{3}{13}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+\\left(\\frac{9}{26}\\right)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186305116 :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n3\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}-9{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{3x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x).$ So, we assume the particular solution to be \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=-4\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)+4\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n3(-4\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)+4\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x))-9(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x))=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x).\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n(12\\beta -9\\alpha )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)+(-12\\alpha -9\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x).\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}12\\beta -9\\alpha &=6\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)) \\\\ -12\\alpha -9\\beta &=0\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x))\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{6}{25},\\beta =\\frac{8}{25}.$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-\\frac{6}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)+\\frac{8}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x).\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{3x}+\\left(-\\frac{6}{25}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)+\\left(\\frac{8}{25}\\right)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x).\n$$" "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n3\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}-9{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{3x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x).$ So, we assume the particular solution to be \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=-4\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)+4\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n3(-4\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)+4\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x))-9(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x))=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x).\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n(12\\beta -9\\alpha )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)+(-12\\alpha -9\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x).\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}12\\beta -9\\alpha &=6\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)) \\\\ -12\\alpha -9\\beta &=0\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x))\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $$\n\\displaystyle \\alpha =-\\frac{6}{25},\\beta =\\frac{8}{25}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-\\frac{6}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)+\\frac{8}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x).\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{3x}+\\left(-\\frac{6}{25}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)+\\left(\\frac{8}{25}\\right)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186305126 :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=2x(t)+y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=2,$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are ${v}_{1}=[1,\\text{ }-1]^{T}$ and ${v}_{2}=[1,\\text{ }2]^{T},$ respectively. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.The general solution of the system is\n\n$