egin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix}\\,+$\n{{field-2}}\n$\\,\\cdot \\,\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}{t}^{2}-1 \\\\ -2t+3\\end{bmatrix}.$" "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}x(t)+\\begin{bmatrix}-2t+6 \\\\ -2{t}^{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFind the missing coefficients in the solution to the initial value problem, shown above.\n\n$x(t)=$ \n{{field-1}} \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\,\\cdot \\,\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix}\\,+\n$$\n{{field-2}}\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\,\\cdot \\,\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}{t}^{2}-1 \\\\ -2t+3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186308479 :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}x(t)+\\begin{bmatrix}-14\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-15\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-11\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{bmatrix},\\,x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFind the missing coefficients in the solution to the initial value problem, shown above.\n\n$x(t)=$ \n{{field-1}} \n$\\,\\cdot \\,\\begin{bmatrix}2{e}^{-2t} \\\\ {e}^{-2t}\\end{bmatrix}\\,+$\n{{field-2}}\n$\\,\\cdot \\,\\begin{bmatrix}(2t-1){e}^{-2t} \\\\ t{e}^{-2t}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{bmatrix}.$" "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}x(t)+\\begin{bmatrix}-14\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-15\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-11\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{bmatrix},\\,x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFind the missing coefficients in the solution to the initial value problem, shown above.\n\n$x(t)=$ \n{{field-1}} \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\,\\cdot \\,\\begin{bmatrix}2{e}^{-2t} \\\\ {e}^{-2t}\\end{bmatrix}\\,+\n$$\n{{field-2}}\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\,\\cdot \\,\\begin{bmatrix}(2t-1){e}^{-2t} \\\\ t{e}^{-2t}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186308480 :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}x(t)+\\begin{bmatrix}7\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ -5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix},\\,x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFind the missing coefficients in the solution to the initial value problem, shown above.\n\n$x(t)=$ \n{{field-1}} \n$\\,\\cdot \\,\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t){e}^{-t} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t){e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix}\\,-$\n{{field-2}}\n$\\,\\cdot \\,\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t){e}^{-t} \\\\ -\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t){e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix}.$" "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}x(t)+\\begin{bmatrix}7\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ -5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix},\\,x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFind the missing coefficients in the solution to the initial value problem, shown above.\n\n$x(t)=$ \n{{field-1}} \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\,\\cdot \\,\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t){e}^{-t} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t){e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix}\\,-\n$$\n{{field-2}}\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\,\\cdot \\,\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t){e}^{-t} \\\\ -\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t){e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186308481 :question/worked-solution "The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}={c}_{1}{e}^{4t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{-2t}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\n- The line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=x.\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{4t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on that line and approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, $y=x$ is $\\text{an unstable}$ line.\n\n- The line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=-x.\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{-2t}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on that line and approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, $y=-x$ is $\\text{a stable}$ line.\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point (origin) is $\\text{a saddle point}.$ \n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/a0/a09d50fa36b2748fb1c65ed16e20d950afbbabc18884ff8718cf1571c4e13b0e.png)" "The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}={c}_{1}{e}^{4t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{-2t}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\n- The line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=x.\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{1}{e}^{4t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nlie on that line and approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, $y=x$ is $\\text{an unstable}$ line.\n\n- The line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=-x.\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{2}{e}^{-2t}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nlie on that line and approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, $y=-x$ is $\\text{a stable}$ line.\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point (origin) is $\\text{a saddle point}.$ \n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/a0/a09d50fa36b2748fb1c65ed16e20d950afbbabc18884ff8718cf1571c4e13b0e.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186308482 :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}x(t)+\\begin{bmatrix}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{bmatrix},\\,x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFind the missing coefficients in the solution to the initial value problem, shown above.\n\n$x(t)=$ \n{{field-1}} \n$\\,\\cdot \\,\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix}\\,+$\n{{field-2}}\n$\\,\\cdot \\,\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{bmatrix}.$" "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}x(t)+\\begin{bmatrix}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{bmatrix},\\,x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFind the missing coefficients in the solution to the initial value problem, shown above.\n\n$x(t)=$ \n{{field-1}} \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\,\\cdot \\,\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix}\\,+\n$$\n{{field-2}}\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\,\\cdot \\,\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186308483 :question/worked-solution "The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}={c}_{1}{e}^{t}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{2t}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\n- The line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=-x.\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{t}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on that line and approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, $y=-x$ is $\\text{an unstable}$ line.\n\n- The line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=-2x.\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{2t}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on that line and approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, $y=-2x$ is $\\text{an unstable}$ line.\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point (origin) is $\\text{an unstable node (source)}.$ \n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/45/450d43a4cbf6dad8d54edbb66fc4a6e44e491290e1dc5d1303057fb0eb7928a7.png)" "The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}={c}_{1}{e}^{t}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{2t}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\n- The line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=-x.\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{1}{e}^{t}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nlie on that line and approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, $y=-x$ is $\\text{an unstable}$ line.\n\n- The line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=-2x.\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{2}{e}^{2t}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nlie on that line and approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, $y=-2x$ is $\\text{an unstable}$ line.\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point (origin) is $\\text{an unstable node (source)}.$ \n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/45/450d43a4cbf6dad8d54edbb66fc4a6e44e491290e1dc5d1303057fb0eb7928a7.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186308485 :question/worked-solution "The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}={c}_{1}{e}^{-3t}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{-6t}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\n- The line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{2}x.\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{-3t}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on that line and approach the origin as as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, $y=\\frac{1}{2}x$ is $\\text{a stable}$ line.\n\n- The line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=-x.\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{-6t}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on that line and approach the origin as as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, $y=-x$ is $\\text{a stable}$ line.\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point (origin) is $\\text{a stable node (sink)}.$ \n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/a2/a21b978aaab9569ae4dcae346116e51b5248fdf8f3cff461411a2140f70a6a4c.png)" "The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}={c}_{1}{e}^{-3t}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{-6t}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\n- The line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{2}x.\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{1}{e}^{-3t}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nlie on that line and approach the origin as as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, $y=\\frac{1}{2}x$ is $\\text{a stable}$ line.\n\n- The line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=-x.\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{2}{e}^{-6t}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nlie on that line and approach the origin as as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, $y=-x$ is $\\text{a stable}$ line.\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point (origin) is $\\text{a stable node (sink)}.$ \n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/a2/a21b978aaab9569ae4dcae346116e51b5248fdf8f3cff461411a2140f70a6a4c.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186308496 :question/worked-solution "For a second-order initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=f(x,y,{y}^{′}),\\,y(a)=c,\\,{y}^{′}(a)=d,\n$$\n\nwe can use Euler's method to approximate a solution by first reducing the second-order ODE to a system of first-order ODEs,\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} &=z,\\, && y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{′} &=f(x,y,z)\\, && z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nbefore applying the update rule component-wise to each equation.\n\nTo apply Euler’s method, we first isolate ${y}^{″}$ so it can serve as the derivative of a new variable $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x{y}^{″}-3y{y}^{′} & =2{x}^{2} \\\\ x{y}^{″} & =2{x}^{2}+3y{y}^{′} \\\\ {y}^{″} & =\\frac{2{x}^{2}+3y{y}^{′}}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variable $z={y}^{′},{z}^{′}={y}^{″}$ in the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{z}^{′}=\\frac{2{x}^{2}+3yz}{x}.\n$$\n\nNote that the initial value of $z$ is\n$z(1)={y}^{′}(1)=1.$\nSo, the initial value problem reduces to the following system of first-order ODEs:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} &=z,\\, && y(1)=-1, \\\\ {z}^{′} &=\\frac{2{x}^{2}+3yz}{x},\\, && z(1)=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, we can proceed with Euler's method by applying the update rule component-wise.\n\nSince we want to find the values of $y$ and ${y}^{′}=z$ at $x=\\frac{3}{2},$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{3}{2}-1=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we place the initial condition data in the table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ |  |  | $1$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute both ${y}^{′}$ and ${z}^{′}$ according to the given rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =z=1 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =\\frac{2{x}^{2}+3yz}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot {1}^{2}+3\\cdot (-1)\\cdot 1}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2-3}{1} \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $1$ |  | $1$ | $-1$ |  |\n\nNext, we compute $Δy$ and $Δz$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n\nTo get the new values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x,$ $Δy$ to $y,$ and $Δz$ to $z,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=-1+\\frac{1}{2}=-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=1+\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these values to our table in a new row gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n| $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ |  |  | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)≈-\\frac{1}{2}$\nand\n${y}^{′}\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)=z\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)≈\\frac{1}{2}.$" "For a second-order initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=f(x,y,{y}^{′}),\\,y(a)=c,\\,{y}^{′}(a)=d,\n$$\n\nwe can use Euler's method to approximate a solution by first reducing the second-order ODE to a system of first-order ODEs,\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} &=z,\\, && y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{′} &=f(x,y,z)\\, && z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nbefore applying the update rule component-wise to each equation.\n\nTo apply Euler’s method, we first isolate ${y}^{″}$ so it can serve as the derivative of a new variable $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x{y}^{″}-3y{y}^{′} & =2{x}^{2} \\\\ x{y}^{″} & =2{x}^{2}+3y{y}^{′} \\\\ {y}^{″} & =\\frac{2{x}^{2}+3y{y}^{′}}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variable $z={y}^{′},{z}^{′}={y}^{″}$ in the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{z}^{′}=\\frac{2{x}^{2}+3yz}{x}.\n$$\n\nNote that the initial value of $z$ is\n$z(1)={y}^{′}(1)=1.$\nSo, the initial value problem reduces to the following system of first-order ODEs:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} &=z,\\, && y(1)=-1, \\\\ {z}^{′} &=\\frac{2{x}^{2}+3yz}{x},\\, && z(1)=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, we can proceed with Euler's method by applying the update rule component-wise.\n\nSince we want to find the values of $y$ and ${y}^{′}=z$ at $x=\\frac{3}{2},$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{3}{2}-1=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we place the initial condition data in the table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ |  |  | $1$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute both ${y}^{′}$ and ${z}^{′}$ according to the given rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =z=1 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =\\frac{2{x}^{2}+3yz}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot {1}^{2}+3\\cdot (-1)\\cdot 1}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2-3}{1} \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $1$ |  | $1$ | $-1$ |  |\n\nNext, we compute $Δy$ and $Δz$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n\nTo get the new values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x,$ $Δy$ to $y,$ and $Δz$ to $z,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=-1+\\frac{1}{2}=-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=1+\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these values to our table in a new row gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n| $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ |  |  | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)≈-\\frac{1}{2}$\nand\n$$\n\\displaystyle {y}^{′}\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)=z\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)≈\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186308515 :question/worked-solution "The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{4t}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nLet's now sketch the phase portrait.\n\n- First, notice that ${x}_{E}(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ are the equilibrium solutions of the system since ${x}_{E}^{′}(t)=0.$ These solutions form the line with the equation $y=x$ in the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](images/18/18fad31b9f69e55d9f4bc414d3e1b732eaee7685bf36a183033fc302e911eb82.png)\n\n- Now, the line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation \n$y=-3x$\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{4t}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on that line. Since ${e}^{4t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n![](images/2b/2bfda58fee727ccdc9f424140e2b8143bf5afe426ef0b1fa72105272d9a1b41e.png)\n\nAll other non-equilibrium solutions are parallel to the vector ${v}_{2}.$ So, the phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/f0/f0821717299fb0b9858409db9a93a610a75e0564a2b60f5bfcd6eca26d2909ec.png)" "The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{4t}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nLet's now sketch the phase portrait.\n\n- First, notice that ${x}_{E}(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ are the equilibrium solutions of the system since ${x}_{E}^{′}(t)=0.$ These solutions form the line with the equation $y=x$ in the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](images/18/18fad31b9f69e55d9f4bc414d3e1b732eaee7685bf36a183033fc302e911eb82.png)\n\n- Now, the line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation \n$y=-3x$\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{2}{e}^{4t}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nlie on that line. Since ${e}^{4t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n![](images/2b/2bfda58fee727ccdc9f424140e2b8143bf5afe426ef0b1fa72105272d9a1b41e.png)\n\nAll other non-equilibrium solutions are parallel to the vector ${v}_{2}.$ So, the phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/f0/f0821717299fb0b9858409db9a93a610a75e0564a2b60f5bfcd6eca26d2909ec.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186308523 :question/problem "If $G(x)={\\int }_{x}^{\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)}(1+{t}^{2})^{2}\\,\\text{d}t,$ then ${G}^{′}(0)=${{field-1}}." "If $$\n\\displaystyle G(x)={\\int }_{x}^{\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)}(1+{t}^{2})^{2}\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$ then ${G}^{′}(0)=${{field-1}}."] [:db/cas 17592186308530 :question/worked-solution "The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{4t}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nLet's now sketch the phase portrait.\n\n- First, notice that ${x}_{E}(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ are the equilibrium solutions of the system since ${x}_{E}^{′}(t)=0.$ These solutions form the line with the equation $y=-\\frac{1}{2}x$ in the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](images/41/41fff6a9cacaf140a6f86b498ba9415aef0a4699fcb119146ee49e0c98843eeb.png)\n\n- Now, the line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation \n$y=\\frac{1}{2}x$\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{4t}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on that line. Since ${e}^{4t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n![](images/34/3499c19ed90a1cbd1916878fe2b9b26401c4eedd656cb9026fbdf35e5b78acb4.png)\n\nAll other non-equilibrium solutions are parallel to the vector ${v}_{2}.$ So, the phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/12/126db28b161fa823ae5048f79f0ed4516cf3b24053f83cbcd9bc564ae11cd3ef.png)" "The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{4t}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nLet's now sketch the phase portrait.\n\n- First, notice that ${x}_{E}(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ are the equilibrium solutions of the system since ${x}_{E}^{′}(t)=0.$ These solutions form the line with the equation $y=-\\frac{1}{2}x$ in the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](images/41/41fff6a9cacaf140a6f86b498ba9415aef0a4699fcb119146ee49e0c98843eeb.png)\n\n- Now, the line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation \n$y=\\frac{1}{2}x$\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{2}{e}^{4t}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nlie on that line. Since ${e}^{4t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n![](images/34/3499c19ed90a1cbd1916878fe2b9b26401c4eedd656cb9026fbdf35e5b78acb4.png)\n\nAll other non-equilibrium solutions are parallel to the vector ${v}_{2}.$ So, the phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/12/126db28b161fa823ae5048f79f0ed4516cf3b24053f83cbcd9bc564ae11cd3ef.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186308531 :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and associated eigenvectors of the matrix \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 18 \\\\ -8 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWe are given that the eigenvalue is ${\\lambda }_{1}=12\\text{i}.$ \n\nLet's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors. For ${\\lambda }_{1}=12\\text{i},$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{1}I=\\begin{bmatrix}-12\\text{i} & 18 \\\\ -8 & -12\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3\\text{i} \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Note:** The eigenvector corresponding to the second eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{1}$ is \n${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}.$ But we do not require this eigenvector for solving our system.\n\nThe solution that we obtain for the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=12\\text{i}$ and its associated eigenvector is\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-3\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{\\text{12i}t}.\n$$\n\nWe now express this as a real function. Using Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{\\text{12i}t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡(12t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12t)) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3\\text{i}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(12t)+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12t) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(12t)+2\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12t)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12t)-3\\text{i}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(12t) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(12t)+2\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12t)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12t) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(12t)\\end{bmatrix}+\\text{i}\\begin{bmatrix}-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(12t) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12t)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+\\text{i}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${w}_{1}$ and ${w}_{2}$ are linearly independent. Therefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & ={c}_{1}{\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12t) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(12t)\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(12t) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12t)\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\nf(t)=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12t),\\,g(t)=-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(12t).\n$$" "We need to find the eigenvalues and associated eigenvectors of the matrix \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 18 \\\\ -8 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWe are given that the eigenvalue is ${\\lambda }_{1}=12\\text{i}.$ \n\nLet's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors. For ${\\lambda }_{1}=12\\text{i},$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{1}I=\\begin{bmatrix}-12\\text{i} & 18 \\\\ -8 & -12\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3\\text{i} \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Note:** The eigenvector corresponding to the second eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{1}$ is \n${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}.$ But we do not require this eigenvector for solving our system.\n\nThe solution that we obtain for the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=12\\text{i}$ and its associated eigenvector is\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-3\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{\\text{12i}t}.\n$$\n\nWe now express this as a real function. Using Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{\\text{12i}t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡(12t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12t)) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3\\text{i}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(12t)+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12t) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(12t)+2\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12t)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12t)-3\\text{i}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(12t) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(12t)+2\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12t)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12t) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(12t)\\end{bmatrix}+\\text{i}\\begin{bmatrix}-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(12t) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12t)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+\\text{i}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${w}_{1}$ and ${w}_{2}$ are linearly independent. Therefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & ={c}_{1}{\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12t) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(12t)\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(12t) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12t)\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\nf(t)=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12t),\\,g(t)=-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(12t).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186308535 :question/problem "If $G(x)={\\int }_{x}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)}{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t,$ then ${G}^{′}(0)=${{field-1}}." "If $$\n\\displaystyle G(x)={\\int }_{x}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)}{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$ then ${G}^{′}(0)=${{field-1}}."] [:db/cas 17592186308536 :question/worked-solution "A numerical method  ${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$ is stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=\\frac{1}{2},\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nWhen a method is applied to the test equation, the update takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $R(\\lambda Δx)$ is the stability function of the method. The set of values for which the method is stable, given by $|R(\\lambda Δx)|<1,$ is called the stability region of the method.\n\nA method that is stable for all possible values $\\lambda Δx>0$ is called absolutely stable.\n\nNow, let’s check the stability of the modified Euler Method by applying it to the test equation  \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-2y,\\,y(0)=1.\n$$\n\nWe now examine our statements:\n\n- Applying the modified Euler method to this test equation, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}^{(p)} & ={y}_{n}+f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={y}_{n}-2{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n+1} & ={y}_{n}+\\frac{1}{2}(f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})+f({x}_{n+1},{y}_{n}-2{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx))\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={y}_{n}+\\frac{1}{2}(-2{y}_{n}-2({y}_{n}-2{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx))\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={y}_{n}+\\frac{1}{2}(-4{y}_{n}+4{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx)\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={y}_{n}-2{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx+2{y}_{n}\\cdot (Δx)^{2} \\\\  & =[1-2Δx+2(Δx)^{2}]{y}_{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the stability function is $R(2Δx)=1-2Δx+2(Δx)^{2}.$ Hence, statement “The stability function is $R(2Δx)=1-2Δx+2(Δx)^{2}.$” is true.\n\n- The method is stable when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(2Δx)| & <1 \\\\ |1-2Δx+2(Δx)^{2}| & <1 \\\\ |2{(Δx-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}| & <1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${y}_{n}$ converges to $0$ as $n\\to \\infty $ if and only if\n\n$$\n|2{(Δx-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}|<1.\n$$\n\nHence, statement “The stability region is $|2{(Δx-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}|<1.$” is true.\n\n- Now, since the argument of the absolute value is always non-negative, we can remove the absolute value. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{(Δx-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2} & <1 \\\\ 2{(Δx-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & <\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {(Δx-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & <\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}<Δx-\\frac{1}{2} & <\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0<Δx & <1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the modified Euler method is not absolutely stable, and statement “The method is absolutely stable” is false.\n\n- Finally, since it satisfies this inequality, the modified Euler method is stable for step size $Δx=\\frac{1}{2},$ and hence statement “The method is stable for step size $Δx=\\frac{1}{2}.$” is true.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is “The stability function is $R(2Δx)=1-2Δx+2(Δx)^{2}.$”; “The stability region is $|2{(Δx-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}|<1.$”; “The method is stable for step size $Δx=\\frac{1}{2}.$” only." "A numerical method  ${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$ is stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=\\frac{1}{2},\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nWhen a method is applied to the test equation, the update takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $R(\\lambda Δx)$ is the stability function of the method. The set of values for which the method is stable, given by $|R(\\lambda Δx)|<1,$ is called the stability region of the method.\n\nA method that is stable for all possible values $\\lambda Δx>0$ is called absolutely stable.\n\nNow, let’s check the stability of the modified Euler Method by applying it to the test equation  \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-2y,\\,y(0)=1.\n$$\n\nWe now examine our statements:\n\n- Applying the modified Euler method to this test equation, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}^{(p)} & ={y}_{n}+f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={y}_{n}-2{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n+1} & ={y}_{n}+\\frac{1}{2}(f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})+f({x}_{n+1},{y}_{n}-2{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx))\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={y}_{n}+\\frac{1}{2}(-2{y}_{n}-2({y}_{n}-2{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx))\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={y}_{n}+\\frac{1}{2}(-4{y}_{n}+4{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx)\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={y}_{n}-2{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx+2{y}_{n}\\cdot (Δx)^{2} \\\\  & =[1-2Δx+2(Δx)^{2}]{y}_{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the stability function is $R(2Δx)=1-2Δx+2(Δx)^{2}.$ Hence, statement “The stability function is $R(2Δx)=1-2Δx+2(Δx)^{2}.$” is true.\n\n- The method is stable when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(2Δx)| & <1 \\\\ |1-2Δx+2(Δx)^{2}| & <1 \\\\ |2{(Δx-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}| & <1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${y}_{n}$ converges to $0$ as $n\\to \\infty $ if and only if\n\n$$\n|2{(Δx-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}|<1.\n$$\n\nHence, statement “The stability region is $$\n\\displaystyle |2{(Δx-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}|<1.\n$$” is true.\n\n- Now, since the argument of the absolute value is always non-negative, we can remove the absolute value. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{(Δx-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2} & <1 \\\\ 2{(Δx-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & <\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {(Δx-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & <\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}<Δx-\\frac{1}{2} & <\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0<Δx & <1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the modified Euler method is not absolutely stable, and statement “The method is absolutely stable” is false.\n\n- Finally, since it satisfies this inequality, the modified Euler method is stable for step size $Δx=\\frac{1}{2},$ and hence statement “The method is stable for step size $Δx=\\frac{1}{2}.$” is true.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is “The stability function is $R(2Δx)=1-2Δx+2(Δx)^{2}.$”; “The stability region is $$\n\\displaystyle |2{(Δx-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}|<1.\n$$”; “The method is stable for step size $Δx=\\frac{1}{2}.$” only."] [:db/cas 17592186308557 :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and associated eigenvectors of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nWe are given that the eigenvalue is ${\\lambda }_{1}=4+\\text{i}.$ \n\nLet's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors. For ${\\lambda }_{1}=4+\\text{i},$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{1}I=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\text{i} & 1 \\\\ -1 & -\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & \\text{i} \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Note:** The eigenvector corresponding to the second eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{1}$ is \n${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}.$ But we do not require this eigenvector for solving our system.\n\nThe solution that we obtain for the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=4+\\text{i}$ and its associated eigenvector is\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{(4+\\text{i})t}.\n$$\n\nWe now express this as a real function. Using Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{(4+\\text{i})t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{4t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{4t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\text{i}({e}^{4t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t) \\\\ {e}^{4t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\text{i}({e}^{4t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{4t}+\\text{i}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{4t} \\\\  & ={\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+\\text{i}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${w}_{1}$ and ${w}_{2}$ are linearly independent. Therefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & ={c}_{1}{\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{4t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{4t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $f(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.$" "We need to find the eigenvalues and associated eigenvectors of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nWe are given that the eigenvalue is ${\\lambda }_{1}=4+\\text{i}.$ \n\nLet's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors. For ${\\lambda }_{1}=4+\\text{i},$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{1}I=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\text{i} & 1 \\\\ -1 & -\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & \\text{i} \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Note:** The eigenvector corresponding to the second eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{1}$ is \n${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}.$ But we do not require this eigenvector for solving our system.\n\nThe solution that we obtain for the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=4+\\text{i}$ and its associated eigenvector is\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{(4+\\text{i})t}.\n$$\n\nWe now express this as a real function. Using Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{(4+\\text{i})t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{4t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{4t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\text{i}({e}^{4t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t) \\\\ {e}^{4t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\text{i}({e}^{4t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{4t}+\\text{i}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{4t} \\\\  & ={\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+\\text{i}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${w}_{1}$ and ${w}_{2}$ are linearly independent. Therefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & ={c}_{1}{\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{4t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{4t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $f(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.$"] [:db/cas 17592186308558 :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and associated eigenvectors of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nWe are given that the eigenvalue is ${\\lambda }_{1}=1+2\\text{i}.$ \n\nLet's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors. For ${\\lambda }_{1}=1+2\\text{i},$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{1}I=\\begin{bmatrix}-2\\text{i} & -2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -\\text{i} \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Note:** The eigenvector corresponding to the second eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{1}$ is \n${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}.$ But we do not require this eigenvector for solving our system.\n\nThe solution that we obtain for the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=1+2\\text{i}$ and its associated eigenvector is\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{(1+2\\text{i})t}.\n$$\n\nWe now express this as a real function. Using Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{(1+2\\text{i})t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+\\text{i}({e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)) \\\\ {e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\text{i}({e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t}+\\text{i}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t} \\\\  & ={\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+\\text{i}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${w}_{1}$ and ${w}_{2}$ are linearly independent. Therefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & ={c}_{1}{\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $f(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t).$" "We need to find the eigenvalues and associated eigenvectors of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nWe are given that the eigenvalue is ${\\lambda }_{1}=1+2\\text{i}.$ \n\nLet's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors. For ${\\lambda }_{1}=1+2\\text{i},$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{1}I=\\begin{bmatrix}-2\\text{i} & -2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -\\text{i} \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Note:** The eigenvector corresponding to the second eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{1}$ is \n${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}.$ But we do not require this eigenvector for solving our system.\n\nThe solution that we obtain for the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=1+2\\text{i}$ and its associated eigenvector is\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{(1+2\\text{i})t}.\n$$\n\nWe now express this as a real function. Using Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{(1+2\\text{i})t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+\\text{i}({e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)) \\\\ {e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\text{i}({e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t}+\\text{i}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t} \\\\  & ={\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+\\text{i}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${w}_{1}$ and ${w}_{2}$ are linearly independent. Therefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & ={c}_{1}{\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $f(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t).$"] [:db/cas 17592186308583 :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and associated eigenvectors of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nWe are given that the eigenvalue is ${\\lambda }_{1}=1+\\text{i}.$ \n\nLet's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors. For ${\\lambda }_{1}=1+\\text{i},$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{1}I=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\text{i} & 1 \\\\ -1 & -\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & \\text{i} \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Note:** The eigenvector corresponding to the second eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{1}$ is \n${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}.$ But we do not require this eigenvector for solving our system.\n\nThe solution that we obtain for the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=1+\\text{i}$ and its associated eigenvector is\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{(1+\\text{i})t}.\n$$\n\nWe now express this as a real function. Using Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{(1+\\text{i})t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\text{i}{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ {e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\text{i}{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t}+\\text{i}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t} \\\\  & ={\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+\\text{i}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${w}_{1}$ and ${w}_{2}$ are linearly independent. Therefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & ={c}_{1}{\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & =1\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t}+0\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We need to find the eigenvalues and associated eigenvectors of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nWe are given that the eigenvalue is ${\\lambda }_{1}=1+\\text{i}.$ \n\nLet's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors. For ${\\lambda }_{1}=1+\\text{i},$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{1}I=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\text{i} & 1 \\\\ -1 & -\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & \\text{i} \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Note:** The eigenvector corresponding to the second eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{1}$ is \n${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}.$ But we do not require this eigenvector for solving our system.\n\nThe solution that we obtain for the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=1+\\text{i}$ and its associated eigenvector is\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{(1+\\text{i})t}.\n$$\n\nWe now express this as a real function. Using Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{(1+\\text{i})t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\text{i}{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ {e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\text{i}{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t}+\\text{i}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname