>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [:db/cas 17592186308690 :question/problem "Suppose that $f(t)$ is piecewise continuous on $[0,\\infty )$, has exponential order $a,$ and its Laplace transform\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{f(t)}\n$$\n\nexists for $s>a.$ Then, $F(s)$ is differentiable at every point $s>a,$ and we have\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(s)=-\\,L{t\\cdot f(t)}.\n$$\n\nA proof of this statement is given below. \n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ $F(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t$ exists for $s>a.$\n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ ${F}^{′}(s)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t$\n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ ${F}^{′}(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{∂}{∂s}({e}^{-st}f(t))\\,\\text{d}t$\n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ $\\frac{∂}{∂s}({e}^{-st}f(t))=-t{e}^{-st}f(t)$\n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,$ ${F}^{′}(s)=-{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }t{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t$\n\n$\\text{L6}:\\,$ ${F}^{′}(s)=-L{t\\cdot f(t)}$\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ The integral in line $\\text{L1}$ converges since\n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L2}$ follows from line $\\text{L1}$ and\n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L3}$ follows from $\\text{L2}$ and {{field-3}}." "Suppose that $f(t)$ is piecewise continuous on $[0,\\infty )$, has exponential order $a,$ and its Laplace transform\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{f(t)}\n$$\n\nexists for $s>a.$ Then, $F(s)$ is differentiable at every point $s>a,$ and we have\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(s)=-\\,L{t\\cdot f(t)}.\n$$\n\nA proof of this statement is given below. \n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ $$\n\\displaystyle F(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$ exists for $s>a.$\n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ $$\n\\displaystyle {F}^{′}(s)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ $$\n\\displaystyle {F}^{′}(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{∂}{∂s}({e}^{-st}f(t))\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ $\\frac{∂}{∂s}({e}^{-st}f(t))=-t{e}^{-st}f(t)$\n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,$ $$\n\\displaystyle {F}^{′}(s)=-{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }t{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\n$\\text{L6}:\\,$ ${F}^{′}(s)=-L{t\\cdot f(t)}$\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ The integral in line $\\text{L1}$ converges since\n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L2}$ follows from line $\\text{L1}$ and\n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L3}$ follows from $\\text{L2}$ and {{field-3}}."] [:db/cas 17592186308692 :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}x+\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{5t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,x(1)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWrite down the expression that gives the solution of the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{5t} & t{e}^{5t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{5t}\\end{bmatrix},$ where $A=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.$*\n\n| $x(t)=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  | $\\,\\,+{\\int }_{1}^{t}\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-2}} |  | $\\,\\,\\text{d}s$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $0$ |  |  | $0$ |  |  |  |  |" "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}x+\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{5t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,x(1)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWrite down the expression that gives the solution of the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{5t} & t{e}^{5t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{5t}\\end{bmatrix},$ where $A=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.$*\n\n| $x(t)=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  | $$\n\\displaystyle \\,\\,+{\\int }_{1}^{t}\\,\\,\n$$ |  | {{field-2}} |  | $\\,\\,\\text{d}s$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $0$ |  |  | $0$ |  |  |  |  |"] [:db/cas 17592186308695 :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}x+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWrite down the expression that gives the solution of the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix},$ where $A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.$*\n\n| $x(t)=\\,\\,$ |  | $3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)$ |  | $\\,\\,+{\\int }_{0}^{t}\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  | $\\,\\,\\text{d}s$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} |  |  | $-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2(t-s))$ |  |  |  |  |" "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}x+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWrite down the expression that gives the solution of the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix},$ where $A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.$*\n\n| $x(t)=\\,\\,$ |  | $3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)$ |  | $$\n\\displaystyle \\,\\,+{\\int }_{0}^{t}\\,\\,\n$$ |  | {{field-1}} |  | $\\,\\,\\text{d}s$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} |  |  | $-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2(t-s))$ |  |  |  |  |"] [:db/cas 17592186308696 :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}x+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWrite down the expression that gives the solution of the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\end{bmatrix},$ where $A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.$*\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$x(t)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"80px\">$5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,\\,+{\\int }_{0}^{t}\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"80px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,\\,\\text{d}s$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4(t-s))$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}x+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWrite down the expression that gives the solution of the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\end{bmatrix},$ where $A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.$*\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$x(t)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"80px\">$5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\displaystyle \\,\\,+{\\int }_{0}^{t}\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"80px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,\\,\\text{d}s$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4(t-s))$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [:db/cas 17592186308702 :question/problem "Suppose that $f(t)$ is piecewise continuous on $[0,\\infty )$, has exponential order $a,$ and its Laplace transform\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{f(t)}\n$$\n\nexists for $s>a.$ Then, $F(s)$ is twice differentiable at every point $s>a,$ and we have\n\n$$\n{F}^{″}(s)=L{{t}^{2}\\cdot f(t)}.\n$$\n\nA proof of this statement is given below. \n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ $F(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt$ exists for $s>a.$\n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ ${F}^{″}(s)=\\frac{{d}^{2}}{d{s}^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt$\n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ ${F}^{″}(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂{s}^{2}}({e}^{-st}f(t))\\,dt$\n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ $\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂{s}^{2}}({e}^{-st}f(t))={t}^{2}{e}^{-st}f(t)$\n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,$ ${F}^{″}(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{t}^{2}{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt$\n\n$\\text{L6}:\\,$ ${F}^{″}(s)=L{{t}^{2}\\cdot f(t)}$\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ The integral in line $\\text{L1}$ converges since\n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L3}$ follows from line $\\text{L2}$ and\n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L5}$ follows from lines $\\text{L3}$ and $\\text{L4}$ by\n{{field-3}}." "Suppose that $f(t)$ is piecewise continuous on $[0,\\infty )$, has exponential order $a,$ and its Laplace transform\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{f(t)}\n$$\n\nexists for $s>a.$ Then, $F(s)$ is twice differentiable at every point $s>a,$ and we have\n\n$$\n{F}^{″}(s)=L{{t}^{2}\\cdot f(t)}.\n$$\n\nA proof of this statement is given below. \n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ $$\n\\displaystyle F(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt\n$$ exists for $s>a.$\n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ $$\n\\displaystyle {F}^{″}(s)=\\frac{{d}^{2}}{d{s}^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt\n$$\n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ $$\n\\displaystyle {F}^{″}(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂{s}^{2}}({e}^{-st}f(t))\\,dt\n$$\n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ $\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂{s}^{2}}({e}^{-st}f(t))={t}^{2}{e}^{-st}f(t)$\n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,$ $$\n\\displaystyle {F}^{″}(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{t}^{2}{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt\n$$\n\n$\\text{L6}:\\,$ ${F}^{″}(s)=L{{t}^{2}\\cdot f(t)}$\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ The integral in line $\\text{L1}$ converges since\n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L3}$ follows from line $\\text{L2}$ and\n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L5}$ follows from lines $\\text{L3}$ and $\\text{L4}$ by\n{{field-3}}."] [:db/cas 17592186308703 :question/worked-solution "If a function $f(t)$ is piecewise continuous on $[0,\\infty )$ and has exponential order $a,$ then its Laplace transform\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{f(t)}\n$$\n\nexists for every real $s>a,$ and $F$ is infinitely differentiable in this region of convergence.\n\nThe smoothness property states that, for each integer $n\\ge 0$ and every $s>a,$\n\n$$\n{F}^{(n)}(s)=(-1)^{n}\\,L{{t}^{n}f(t)}.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “If $f(t)$ is of exponential order $a,$ then the integral ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt$ converges for every $s>a.$” is true. Since $f$ is of exponential order and is piecewise continuous, there exist constants $K,T>0$ and $a$ such that\n\n$$\n|f(t)|\\le K{e}^{at},\\,t>T.\n$$\n\nWhen computing the Laplace transform, we multiply $f(t)$ by ${e}^{-st}.$ If $s>a$, then for all sufficiently large $t$,\n\n$$\n|{e}^{-st}f(t)|\\le K{e}^{-(s-a)t}.\n$$\n\nBecause $s-a>0$, the function ${e}^{-(s-a)t}$ is integrable on $[T,\\infty )$, and\n$f$ is bounded on $[0,T].$\nTherefore, the entire integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt\n$$\n\nconverges.\n\n- Statement “If $f(t)$ is of exponential order $a,$ then its Laplace transform $F(s)$ exists for all $s>a.$” is true. The standard existence theorem for the Laplace transform says that if $f$ is piecewise continuous on $[0,\\infty )$ and of exponential order $a,$ then the improper integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt\n$$\n\nconverges for every $s>a.$ This is exactly what it means for $F(s)$ to exist for all $s>a.$\n\n- Statement “The Laplace transform of any piecewise continuous function of exponential order is infinitely differentiable in its region of convergence” is true. The smoothness theorem guarantees\n\n$$\n{F}^{(n)}(s)=(-1)^{n}\\,L{{t}^{n}f(t)}\n$$\n\nfor every $n\\ge 0$ and each $s>a,$ which shows that $F(s)$ is infinitely differentiable, even if $f(t)$ itself is not.\n\nTherefore, the answer is “If $f(t)$ is of exponential order $a,$ then the integral ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt$ converges for every $s>a.$”; “If $f(t)$ is of exponential order $a,$ then its Laplace transform $F(s)$ exists for all $s>a.$”; “The Laplace transform of any piecewise continuous function of exponential order is infinitely differentiable in its region of convergence”." "If a function $f(t)$ is piecewise continuous on $[0,\\infty )$ and has exponential order $a,$ then its Laplace transform\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{f(t)}\n$$\n\nexists for every real $s>a,$ and $F$ is infinitely differentiable in this region of convergence.\n\nThe smoothness property states that, for each integer $n\\ge 0$ and every $s>a,$\n\n$$\n{F}^{(n)}(s)=(-1)^{n}\\,L{{t}^{n}f(t)}.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “If $f(t)$ is of exponential order $a,$ then the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt\n$$ converges for every $s>a.$” is true. Since $f$ is of exponential order and is piecewise continuous, there exist constants $K,T>0$ and $a$ such that\n\n$$\n|f(t)|\\le K{e}^{at},\\,t>T.\n$$\n\nWhen computing the Laplace transform, we multiply $f(t)$ by ${e}^{-st}.$ If $s>a$, then for all sufficiently large $t$,\n\n$$\n|{e}^{-st}f(t)|\\le K{e}^{-(s-a)t}.\n$$\n\nBecause $s-a>0$, the function ${e}^{-(s-a)t}$ is integrable on $[T,\\infty )$, and\n$f$ is bounded on $[0,T].$\nTherefore, the entire integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt\n$$\n\nconverges.\n\n- Statement “If $f(t)$ is of exponential order $a,$ then its Laplace transform $F(s)$ exists for all $s>a.$” is true. The standard existence theorem for the Laplace transform says that if $f$ is piecewise continuous on $[0,\\infty )$ and of exponential order $a,$ then the improper integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt\n$$\n\nconverges for every $s>a.$ This is exactly what it means for $F(s)$ to exist for all $s>a.$\n\n- Statement “The Laplace transform of any piecewise continuous function of exponential order is infinitely differentiable in its region of convergence” is true. The smoothness theorem guarantees\n\n$$\n{F}^{(n)}(s)=(-1)^{n}\\,L{{t}^{n}f(t)}\n$$\n\nfor every $n\\ge 0$ and each $s>a,$ which shows that $F(s)$ is infinitely differentiable, even if $f(t)$ itself is not.\n\nTherefore, the answer is “If $f(t)$ is of exponential order $a,$ then the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt\n$$ converges for every $s>a.$”; “If $f(t)$ is of exponential order $a,$ then its Laplace transform $F(s)$ exists for all $s>a.$”; “The Laplace transform of any piecewise continuous function of exponential order is infinitely differentiable in its region of convergence”."] [:db/cas 17592186308705 :question/problem "Given that $I(t)={\\int }_{0}^{2}(x-4{t}^{3})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x,$ calculate ${I}^{′}(t)$ for $t\\in R$ by differentiating under the integral sign.\n\n${I}^{′}(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" "Given that $$\n\\displaystyle I(t)={\\int }_{0}^{2}(x-4{t}^{3})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$ calculate ${I}^{′}(t)$ for $t\\in R$ by differentiating under the integral sign.\n\n${I}^{′}(t)=$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186308706 :question/problem "Given that $K(t)={\\int }_{1}^{2}({x}^{2}+{t}^{3})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x,$ calculate ${K}^{′}(t)$ for $t\\in R$ by differentiating under the integral sign.\n\n${K}^{′}(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" "Given that $$\n\\displaystyle K(t)={\\int }_{1}^{2}({x}^{2}+{t}^{3})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$ calculate ${K}^{′}(t)$ for $t\\in R$ by differentiating under the integral sign.\n\n${K}^{′}(t)=$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186308707 :question/problem "Given that $Q(t)={\\int }_{-1}^{1}({x}^{2}+x+{t}^{3})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x,$ calculate ${Q}^{′}(t)$ for $t\\in R$ by differentiating under the integral sign.\n\n${Q}^{′}(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" "Given that $$\n\\displaystyle Q(t)={\\int }_{-1}^{1}({x}^{2}+x+{t}^{3})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$ calculate ${Q}^{′}(t)$ for $t\\in R$ by differentiating under the integral sign.\n\n${Q}^{′}(t)=$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186308708 :question/problem "Given that $I(t)={\\int }_{-1}^{0}(3{x}^{3}+{t}^{2})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x,$ calculate ${I}^{′}(t)$ for $t\\in R$ by differentiating under the integral sign.\n\n${I}^{′}(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" "Given that $$\n\\displaystyle I(t)={\\int }_{-1}^{0}(3{x}^{3}+{t}^{2})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$ calculate ${I}^{′}(t)$ for $t\\in R$ by differentiating under the integral sign.\n\n${I}^{′}(t)=$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186308713 :question/problem "Given that $K(t)={\\int }_{-2}^{3}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-2x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x,$ calculate ${K}^{′}(t)$ for $t\\in R$ by differentiating under the integral sign.\n\n${K}^{′}(t)=10\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)(\\,$\n{{field-1}}$)$" "Given that $$\n\\displaystyle K(t)={\\int }_{-2}^{3}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-2x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$ calculate ${K}^{′}(t)$ for $t\\in R$ by differentiating under the integral sign.\n\n${K}^{′}(t)=10\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)(\\,$\n{{field-1}}$)$"] [:db/cas 17592186308714 :question/problem "Given that $K(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x+{e}^{t})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x,$ calculate ${K}^{′}(t)$ for $t\\in R$ by differentiating under the integral sign.\n\n${K}^{′}(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" "Given that $$\n\\displaystyle K(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x+{e}^{t})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$ calculate ${K}^{′}(t)$ for $t\\in R$ by differentiating under the integral sign.\n\n${K}^{′}(t)=$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186311549 :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each function:\n\n- For the first function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(x,t)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{|x-t|}}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in (0,1)$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d$ such that $[c,d]⊆(0,1),$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{1}$ is undefined when $x=t,$ since the denominator vanishes. Thus, ${f}_{1}$ is not continuous on $R,$ and differentiating under the integral sign is *not* valid.\n\n- For the second function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{2}(x,t)=\\frac{x}{1+{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in R$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{2}$ is continuous on $R.$ Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂t}=-\\frac{2tx}{(1+{t}^{2})^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{2}^{′}(t)$ for all real $t.$\n\n- For the third function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{3}(x,t)=\\frac{1}{1+x+t}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in (0,1)$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d$ such that $[c,d]⊂(0,1),$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{3}$ is continuous on $R$ (since the denominator never vanishes on $R$). Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂t}=-\\frac{1}{(1+x+t)^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{3}^{′}(t)$ for all $t\\in (0,1).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${F}_{2}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{x}{1+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in R$; ${F}_{3}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{1+x+t}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in (0,1)$ only." "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each function:\n\n- For the first function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(x,t)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{|x-t|}}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in (0,1)$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d$ such that $[c,d]⊆(0,1),$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{1}$ is undefined when $x=t,$ since the denominator vanishes. Thus, ${f}_{1}$ is not continuous on $R,$ and differentiating under the integral sign is *not* valid.\n\n- For the second function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{2}(x,t)=\\frac{x}{1+{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in R$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{2}$ is continuous on $R.$ Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂t}=-\\frac{2tx}{(1+{t}^{2})^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{2}^{′}(t)$ for all real $t.$\n\n- For the third function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{3}(x,t)=\\frac{1}{1+x+t}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in (0,1)$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d$ such that $[c,d]⊂(0,1),$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{3}$ is continuous on $R$ (since the denominator never vanishes on $R$). Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂t}=-\\frac{1}{(1+x+t)^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{3}^{′}(t)$ for all $t\\in (0,1).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle {F}_{2}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{x}{1+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in R;\n$$ $$\n\\displaystyle {F}_{3}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{1+x+t}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in (0,1)\n$$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186311550 :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each function:\n\n- For the first function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(x,t)=\\sqrt{x+t}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}>0$ and choose $[c,d]⊂(0,\\infty )$ such that $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{1}$ is continuous on $R.$ Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂t}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x+t}},\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R$ (since $x+t\\ge c>0$ on $R$). Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{1}^{′}(t)$ for all real $t>0.$\n\n- For the second function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{2}(x,t)=\\frac{xt}{{x}^{2}+{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}>0$ and choose $[c,d]⊂(0,\\infty )$ such that $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{2}$ is continuous on $R$ (since the denominator never vanishes on $R$). Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂t}=\\frac{x({x}^{2}+{t}^{2})-2x{t}^{2}}{({x}^{2}+{t}^{2})^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{2}^{′}(t)$ for all real $t>0.$\n\n- For the third function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{3}(x,t)=\\frac{1}{x-t}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}>1$ and choose $[c,d]⊂(1,\\infty )$ such that $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that for $t>1$ and $x\\in [0,1],$ the denominator $x-t$ never vanishes on $R,$ so ${f}_{3}$ is continuous on $R.$ Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂t}=\\frac{1}{(x-t)^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{3}^{′}(t)$ for all real $t>1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${F}_{1}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{x+t}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t>0$; ${F}_{2}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{xt}{{x}^{2}+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t>0$; ${F}_{3}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{x-t}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t>1$." "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each function:\n\n- For the first function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(x,t)=\\sqrt{x+t}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}>0$ and choose $[c,d]⊂(0,\\infty )$ such that $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{1}$ is continuous on $R.$ Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂t}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x+t}},\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R$ (since $x+t\\ge c>0$ on $R$). Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{1}^{′}(t)$ for all real $t>0.$\n\n- For the second function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{2}(x,t)=\\frac{xt}{{x}^{2}+{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}>0$ and choose $[c,d]⊂(0,\\infty )$ such that $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{2}$ is continuous on $R$ (since the denominator never vanishes on $R$). Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂t}=\\frac{x({x}^{2}+{t}^{2})-2x{t}^{2}}{({x}^{2}+{t}^{2})^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{2}^{′}(t)$ for all real $t>0.$\n\n- For the third function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{3}(x,t)=\\frac{1}{x-t}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}>1$ and choose $[c,d]⊂(1,\\infty )$ such that $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that for $t>1$ and $x\\in [0,1],$ the denominator $x-t$ never vanishes on $R,$ so ${f}_{3}$ is continuous on $R.$ Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂t}=\\frac{1}{(x-t)^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{3}^{′}(t)$ for all real $t>1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle {F}_{1}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{x+t}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t>0;\n$$ $$\n\\displaystyle {F}_{2}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{xt}{{x}^{2}+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t>0;\n$$ $$\n\\displaystyle {F}_{3}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{x-t}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t>1.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186311551 :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each function:\n\n- For the first function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(x,t)={x}^{2}t{e}^{-xt}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}>0$ and choose $0<c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{1}$ is continuous on $R.$ Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂t}={x}^{2}{e}^{-xt}-{x}^{3}t{e}^{-xt}\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{1}^{′}(t)$ for all $t>0.$\n\n- For the second function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{2}(x,t)=\\frac{1}{|x-t|}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in (0,1)$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d$ such that $[c,d]⊆(0,1),$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{2}$ is undefined when $x=t,$ since the denominator vanishes. Thus, ${f}_{2}$ is not continuous on $R,$ and differentiating under the integral sign is *not* valid.\n\n- For the third function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{3}(x,t)=\\frac{1}{(x-t)^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in (0,1)$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d$ such that $[c,d]⊆[0,1],$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{3}$ is continuous on $R$ because the denominator is always positive. Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂t}=\\frac{2(x-t)}{{((x-t)^{2}+1)}^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{3}^{′}(t)$ for all $t\\in [0,1].$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${F}_{1}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}t{e}^{-xt}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t>0$; ${F}_{3}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{(x-t)^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in [0,1]$ only." "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each function:\n\n- For the first function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(x,t)={x}^{2}t{e}^{-xt}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}>0$ and choose $0<c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{1}$ is continuous on $R.$ Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂t}={x}^{2}{e}^{-xt}-{x}^{3}t{e}^{-xt}\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{1}^{′}(t)$ for all $t>0.$\n\n- For the second function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{2}(x,t)=\\frac{1}{|x-t|}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in (0,1)$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d$ such that $[c,d]⊆(0,1),$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{2}$ is undefined when $x=t,$ since the denominator vanishes. Thus, ${f}_{2}$ is not continuous on $R,$ and differentiating under the integral sign is *not* valid.\n\n- For the third function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{3}(x,t)=\\frac{1}{(x-t)^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in (0,1)$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d$ such that $[c,d]⊆[0,1],$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{3}$ is continuous on $R$ because the denominator is always positive. Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂t}=\\frac{2(x-t)}{{((x-t)^{2}+1)}^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{3}^{′}(t)$ for all $t\\in [0,1].$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle {F}_{1}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}t{e}^{-xt}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t>0;\n$$ $$\n\\displaystyle {F}_{3}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{(x-t)^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in [0,1]\n$$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186311552 :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each function:\n\n- For the first function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(x,t)=\\frac{{t}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}>0$ and choose $[c,d]⊂(0,\\infty )$ such that $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{1}$ is continuous on $R$ because the denominator is always positive. Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂t}=\\frac{2t({x}^{2}+{t}^{2})-2{t}^{3}}{({x}^{2}+{t}^{2})^{2}}=\\frac{2t{x}^{2}}{({x}^{2}+{t}^{2})^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ So, differentiating under the integral sign is valid for all $t>0.$\n\n- For the second function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{2}(x,t)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{|x-t|}}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in (0,1)$ and choose $[c,d]⊂(0,1)$ such that $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that this function is undefined when $x=t,$ since the denominator vanishes. Thus, ${f}_{2}$ is not continuous on $R,$ and differentiating under the integral sign is *not* valid.\n\n- For the third function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{3}(x,t)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+{t}^{2}).\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}>0$ and choose $[c,d]⊂(0,\\infty )$ such that $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nSince $x\\in [0,1]$ and $t>0,$ we have $x+{t}^{2}>0,$ so ${f}_{3}$ is continuous. Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂t}=\\frac{2t}{x+{t}^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid for all $t>0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${F}_{1}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{{t}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t>0$; ${F}_{3}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t>0$ only." "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each function:\n\n- For the first function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(x,t)=\\frac{{t}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}>0$ and choose $[c,d]⊂(0,\\infty )$ such that $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{1}$ is continuous on $R$ because the denominator is always positive. Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂t}=\\frac{2t({x}^{2}+{t}^{2})-2{t}^{3}}{({x}^{2}+{t}^{2})^{2}}=\\frac{2t{x}^{2}}{({x}^{2}+{t}^{2})^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ So, differentiating under the integral sign is valid for all $t>0.$\n\n- For the second function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{2}(x,t)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{|x-t|}}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in (0,1)$ and choose $[c,d]⊂(0,1)$ such that $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that this function is undefined when $x=t,$ since the denominator vanishes. Thus, ${f}_{2}$ is not continuous on $R,$ and differentiating under the integral sign is *not* valid.\n\n- For the third function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{3}(x,t)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+{t}^{2}).\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}>0$ and choose $[c,d]⊂(0,\\infty )$ such that $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nSince $x\\in [0,1]$ and $t>0,$ we have $x+{t}^{2}>0,$ so ${f}_{3}$ is continuous. Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂t}=\\frac{2t}{x+{t}^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid for all $t>0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle {F}_{1}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{{t}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t>0;\n$$ $$\n\\displaystyle {F}_{3}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t>0\n$$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186311554 :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each function:\n\n- For the first function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(x,t)=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+tx)}{1+{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in (-1,1)$ and choose $[c,d]⊂(-1,1)$ such that $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{1}$ is continuous on $R$ (since $1+tx>0$). Computing the  partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂t}=\\frac{x}{(1+tx)(1+{x}^{2})}\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{1}^{′}(t)$ for all $t\\in (-1,1).$\n\n- For the second function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{2}(x,t)=\\frac{1}{x-t}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in (1,2)$ and choose $[c,d]⊂(1,2)$ such that $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{2}$ is continuous on $R$ (since the denominator never vanishes on $R$). Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂t}=\\frac{1}{(x-t)^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{2}^{′}(t)$ for all $t\\in (1,2).$\n\n- For the third function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{3}(x,t)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{|x-t|}}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in (0,1)$ and choose $[c,d]⊂(0,1)$ such that $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that this function is undefined when $x=t,$ since the denominator vanishes. Thus, ${f}_{3}$ is not continuous on $R,$ and differentiating under the integral sign is *not* valid.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${F}_{1}(t)$ $={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+tx)}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x,$ $\\,t\\in (-1,1)$; ${F}_{2}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{x-t}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in (1,2)$ only." "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each function:\n\n- For the first function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(x,t)=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+tx)}{1+{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in (-1,1)$ and choose $[c,d]⊂(-1,1)$ such that $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{1}$ is continuous on $R$ (since $1+tx>0$). Computing the  partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂t}=\\frac{x}{(1+tx)(1+{x}^{2})}\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{1}^{′}(t)$ for all $t\\in (-1,1).$\n\n- For the second function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{2}(x,t)=\\frac{1}{x-t}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in (1,2)$ and choose $[c,d]⊂(1,2)$ such that $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{2}$ is continuous on $R$ (since the denominator never vanishes on $R$). Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂t}=\\frac{1}{(x-t)^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{2}^{′}(t)$ for all $t\\in (1,2).$\n\n- For the third function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{3}(x,t)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{|x-t|}}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in (0,1)$ and choose $[c,d]⊂(0,1)$ such that $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that this function is undefined when $x=t,$ since the denominator vanishes. Thus, ${f}_{3}$ is not continuous on $R,$ and differentiating under the integral sign is *not* valid.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${F}_{1}(t)$ $$\n\\displaystyle ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+tx)}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$ $\\,t\\in (-1,1)$; $$\n\\displaystyle {F}_{2}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{x-t}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in (1,2)\n$$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186311555 :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each function:\n\n- For the first function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(x,t)={e}^{-{t}^{2}x}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in R$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{1}$ is continuous on $R.$ Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂t}=-2tx{e}^{-{t}^{2}x},\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{1}^{′}(t)$ for all real $t.$\n\n- For the second function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{2}(x,t)=|x-t|.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in (0,1)$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d$ such that $[c,d]⊆[0,1],$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{2}$ is continuous on $R.$ However, the partial derivative\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂t}={\\begin{aligned}-1,\\,x>t \\\\ 1,\\,x<t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ndoes *not* exist whenever $x=t={t}_{0},$ and is therefore not defined everywhere on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is *not* valid.\n\n- For the third function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{3}(x,t)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}>0$ and choose $[c,d]⊂(0,\\infty )$ such that $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{3}$ is continuous on $R$ (since the denominator never vanishes on $R$). Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂t}=-\\frac{2t}{({x}^{2}+{t}^{2})^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{3}^{′}(t)$ for all $t>0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${F}_{1}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{-{t}^{2}x}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in R$; ${F}_{3}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t>0$ only." "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each function:\n\n- For the first function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(x,t)={e}^{-{t}^{2}x}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in R$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{1}$ is continuous on $R.$ Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂t}=-2tx{e}^{-{t}^{2}x},\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{1}^{′}(t)$ for all real $t.$\n\n- For the second function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{2}(x,t)=|x-t|.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in (0,1)$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d$ such that $[c,d]⊆[0,1],$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{2}$ is continuous on $R.$ However, the partial derivative\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂t}={\\begin{aligned}-1,\\,x>t \\\\ 1,\\,x<t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ndoes *not* exist whenever $x=t={t}_{0},$ and is therefore not defined everywhere on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is *not* valid.\n\n- For the third function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{3}(x,t)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}>0$ and choose $[c,d]⊂(0,\\infty )$ such that $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{3}$ is continuous on $R$ (since the denominator never vanishes on $R$). Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂t}=-\\frac{2t}{({x}^{2}+{t}^{2})^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{3}^{′}(t)$ for all $t>0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle {F}_{1}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{-{t}^{2}x}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in R;\n$$ $$\n\\displaystyle {F}_{3}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t>0\n$$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186311556 :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each function:\n\n- For the first function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(x,t)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{t}^{2}}}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}>0$ and choose $[c,d]⊂(0,\\infty )$ such that $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{1}$ is continuous on $R$ (since ${x}^{2}+{t}^{2}>0$ on $R$). Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂t}=-\\frac{t}{({x}^{2}+{t}^{2})^{3/2}},\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{1}^{′}(t)$ for all $t>0.$\n\n- For the second function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{2}(x,t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xt).\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in R$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d$ so that $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{2}$ is continuous on $R.$ Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂t}=-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xt)\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{2}^{′}(t)$ for all real $t.$\n\n- For the third function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{3}(x,t)=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{1+t{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}>-1$ and choose $[c,d]⊂(-1,\\infty )$ such that $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{3}$ is continuous on $R$ (since the denominator never vanishes on $R$). Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂t}=-\\frac{{x}^{5}}{(1+t{x}^{2})^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{3}^{′}(t)$ for all $t>-1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${F}_{1}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{t}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t>0$; ${F}_{2}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xt)\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in R$; ${F}_{3}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{{x}^{3}}{1+t{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t>-1$." "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each function:\n\n- For the first function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(x,t)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{t}^{2}}}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}>0$ and choose $[c,d]⊂(0,\\infty )$ such that $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{1}$ is continuous on $R$ (since ${x}^{2}+{t}^{2}>0$ on $R$). Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂t}=-\\frac{t}{({x}^{2}+{t}^{2})^{3/2}},\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{1}^{′}(t)$ for all $t>0.$\n\n- For the second function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{2}(x,t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xt).\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in R$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d$ so that $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{2}$ is continuous on $R.$ Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂t}=-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xt)\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{2}^{′}(t)$ for all real $t.$\n\n- For the third function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{3}(x,t)=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{1+t{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}>-1$ and choose $[c,d]⊂(-1,\\infty )$ such that $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{3}$ is continuous on $R$ (since the denominator never vanishes on $R$). Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂t}=-\\frac{{x}^{5}}{(1+t{x}^{2})^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{3}^{′}(t)$ for all $t>-1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle {F}_{1}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{t}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t>0;\n$$ $$\n\\displaystyle {F}_{2}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xt)\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in R;\n$$ $$\n\\displaystyle {F}_{3}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{{x}^{3}}{1+t{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t>-1.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186311557 :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each function:\n\n- For the first function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(x,t)=|x-t|.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in (0,1)$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d$ such that $[c,d]⊆(0,1),$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{1}$ is continuous on $R.$ However, the partial derivative\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂t}={\\begin{aligned}-1,\\,x>t \\\\ 1,\\,x<t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ndoes *not* exist whenever $x=t={t}_{0},$ and is therefore not defined everywhere on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is *not* valid.\n\n- For the second function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{2}(x,t)=\\frac{t}{1+{x}^{2}+{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in R$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{2}$ is continuous on $R.$ Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂t}=\\frac{(1+{x}^{2}+{t}^{2})-2{t}^{2}}{(1+{x}^{2}+{t}^{2})^{2}}=\\frac{1+{x}^{2}-{t}^{2}}{(1+{x}^{2}+{t}^{2})^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{2}^{′}(t)$ for all real $t.$\n\n- For the third function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{3}(x,t)=\\frac{1}{(x+t+1)^{3}}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in (0,1)$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d$ such that $[c,d]⊆(0,1),$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{3}$ is continuous on $R.$ Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂t}=-\\frac{3}{(x+t+1)^{4}}\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{3}^{′}(t)$ for all $t\\in (0,1).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${F}_{2}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{t}{1+{x}^{2}+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in R$; ${F}_{3}(t)$ $={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{(x+t+1)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x,$ $\\,t\\in (0,1)$ only." "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each function:\n\n- For the first function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(x,t)=|x-t|.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in (0,1)$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d$ such that $[c,d]⊆(0,1),$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{1}$ is continuous on $R.$ However, the partial derivative\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂t}={\\begin{aligned}-1,\\,x>t \\\\ 1,\\,x<t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ndoes *not* exist whenever $x=t={t}_{0},$ and is therefore not defined everywhere on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is *not* valid.\n\n- For the second function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{2}(x,t)=\\frac{t}{1+{x}^{2}+{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in R$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{2}$ is continuous on $R.$ Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂t}=\\frac{(1+{x}^{2}+{t}^{2})-2{t}^{2}}{(1+{x}^{2}+{t}^{2})^{2}}=\\frac{1+{x}^{2}-{t}^{2}}{(1+{x}^{2}+{t}^{2})^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{2}^{′}(t)$ for all real $t.$\n\n- For the third function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{3}(x,t)=\\frac{1}{(x+t+1)^{3}}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in (0,1)$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d$ such that $[c,d]⊆(0,1),$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{3}$ is continuous on $R.$ Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂t}=-\\frac{3}{(x+t+1)^{4}}\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{3}^{′}(t)$ for all $t\\in (0,1).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle {F}_{2}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{t}{1+{x}^{2}+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in R;\n$$ ${F}_{3}(t)$ $$\n\\displaystyle ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{(x+t+1)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$ $\\,t\\in (0,1)$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186311558 :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each function:\n\n- For the first function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(x,t)=|x-t|.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in (0,1)$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d$ such that $[c,d]⊆(0,1),$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{1}$ is continuous on $R.$ However, the partial derivative\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂t}={\\begin{aligned}-1,\\,x>t \\\\ 1,\\,x<t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ndoes *not* exist whenever $x=t={t}_{0},$ and is therefore not defined everywhere on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is *not* valid.\n\n- For the second function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{2}(x,t)={e}^{t{x}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in R$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{2}$ is continuous on $R.$ Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂t}={x}^{2}{e}^{t{x}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{2}^{′}(t)$ for all real $t.$\n\n- For the third function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{3}(x,t)=\\frac{x}{x-t}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}<0$ and choose $[c,d]⊂(-\\infty ,0)$ such that $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{3}$ is continuous on $R$ (since the denominator never vanishes on $R$). Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂t}=\\frac{x}{(x-t)^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{3}^{′}(t)$ for all $t<0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${F}_{2}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{t{x}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in R$; ${F}_{3}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{x}{x-t}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t<0$ only." "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each function:\n\n- For the first function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(x,t)=|x-t|.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in (0,1)$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d$ such that $[c,d]⊆(0,1),$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{1}$ is continuous on $R.$ However, the partial derivative\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂t}={\\begin{aligned}-1,\\,x>t \\\\ 1,\\,x<t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ndoes *not* exist whenever $x=t={t}_{0},$ and is therefore not defined everywhere on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is *not* valid.\n\n- For the second function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{2}(x,t)={e}^{t{x}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in R$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{2}$ is continuous on $R.$ Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂t}={x}^{2}{e}^{t{x}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{2}^{′}(t)$ for all real $t.$\n\n- For the third function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{3}(x,t)=\\frac{x}{x-t}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}<0$ and choose $[c,d]⊂(-\\infty ,0)$ such that $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{3}$ is continuous on $R$ (since the denominator never vanishes on $R$). Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂t}=\\frac{x}{(x-t)^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{3}^{′}(t)$ for all $t<0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle {F}_{2}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{t{x}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in R;\n$$ $$\n\\displaystyle {F}_{3}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{x}{x-t}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t<0\n$$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186311573 :question/worked-solution "Euler's method approximates the solution of an initial value problem containing a system of ODEs by applying the update rule component-wise to each equation.\n\nFirst, because the initial conditions are $y(0)=0,$ $z(0)=1,$ and $w(0)=1,$ we place $y=0,$ $z=1,$ and $w=1$ in the table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ | $w$ | ${w}^{′}$ | $Δw$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ |  |  | $1$ |  |  | $1$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′},{z}^{′},$ and ${w}^{′}$ according to the given rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =1+x-2y+3z \\\\  & =1+0-2\\cdot 0+3\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =4 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =2y-w \\\\  & =2\\cdot 0-1 \\\\  & =-1 \\\\ {w}^{′} & =-x+yz \\\\  & =-0+0\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ | $w$ | ${w}^{′}$ | $Δw$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $4$ |  | $1$ | $-1$ |  | $1$ | $0$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy,$ $Δz,$ and $Δw$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =1 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ Δw & ={w}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =0\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ | $w$ | ${w}^{′}$ | $Δw$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $4$ | $1$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-\\frac{1}{4}$ | $1$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n\nTo get the new values of $x,$ $y,$ $z,$ and $w$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x,$ $Δy$ to $y,$ $Δz$ to $z,$ and $Δw$ to $w,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+\\frac{1}{4}=\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=0+1=1 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=1+\\left(-\\frac{1}{4}\\right)=\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ {w}_{\\text{new}} & =w+Δw=1+0=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these values to our table in a new row gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ | $w$ | ${w}^{′}$ | $Δw$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $4$ | $1$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-\\frac{1}{4}$ | $1$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $1$ |  |  | $\\frac{3}{4}$ |  |  | $1$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y\\,\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)≈1,$\n$z\\,\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)≈\\frac{3}{4},$\nand\n$w\\,\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)≈1.$" "Euler's method approximates the solution of an initial value problem containing a system of ODEs by applying the update rule component-wise to each equation.\n\nFirst, because the initial conditions are $y(0)=0,$ $z(0)=1,$ and $w(0)=1,$ we place $y=0,$ $z=1,$ and $w=1$ in the table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ | $w$ | ${w}^{′}$ | $Δw$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ |  |  | $1$ |  |  | $1$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′},{z}^{′},$ and ${w}^{′}$ according to the given rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =1+x-2y+3z \\\\  & =1+0-2\\cdot 0+3\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =4 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =2y-w \\\\  & =2\\cdot 0-1 \\\\  & =-1 \\\\ {w}^{′} & =-x+yz \\\\  & =-0+0\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ | $w$ | ${w}^{′}$ | $Δw$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $4$ |  | $1$ | $-1$ |  | $1$ | $0$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy,$ $Δz,$ and $Δw$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =1 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ Δw & ={w}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =0\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ | $w$ | ${w}^{′}$ | $Δw$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $4$ | $1$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-\\frac{1}{4}$ | $1$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n\nTo get the new values of $x,$ $y,$ $z,$ and $w$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x,$ $Δy$ to $y,$ $Δz$ to $z,$ and $Δw$ to $w,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+\\frac{1}{4}=\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=0+1=1 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=1+\\left(-\\frac{1}{4}\\right)=\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ {w}_{\\text{new}} & =w+Δw=1+0=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these values to our table in a new row gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ | $w$ | ${w}^{′}$ | $Δw$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $4$ | $1$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-\\frac{1}{4}$ | $1$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $1$ |  |  | $\\frac{3}{4}$ |  |  | $1$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y\\,\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)≈1,$\n$$\n\\displaystyle z\\,\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)≈\\frac{3}{4},\n$$\nand\n$w\\,\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)≈1.$"] [:db/cas 17592186311574 :question/worked-solution "Euler's method approximates the solution of an initial value problem containing a system of ODEs by applying the update rule component-wise to each equation.\n\nFirst, because the initial conditions are $y(0)=1,$ $z(0)=-1,$ and $w(0)=0,$ we place $y=1,$ $z=-1,$ and $w=0$ in the table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ | $w$ | ${w}^{′}$ | $Δw$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ |  |  | $-1$ |  |  | $0$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′},{z}^{′},$ and ${w}^{′}$ according to the given rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2-x+z \\\\  & =2-0+(-1) \\\\  & =1 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =3y-2z+w \\\\  & =3\\cdot 1-2\\cdot (-1)+0 \\\\  & =5 \\\\ {w}^{′} & =1-y \\\\  & =1-1 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ | $w$ | ${w}^{′}$ | $Δw$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$