$\n\n$\\text{L7}:\\,$ $$\n\\displaystyle u(t)=\\int {Φ}^{-1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\n$\\text{L8}:\\,$ $$\n\\displaystyle {x}_{p}(t)=Φ\\int {Φ}^{-1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L3}$ is obtained by substituting $\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L2}$ into the {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L6}$ follows from line $\\text{L5}$ by {{field-2}} both sides of the equation.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L7}$ follows from line $\\text{L6}$ by {{field-3}} both sides of the equation.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L8}$ is obtained by substituting $\\text{L7}$ into {{field-4}} ."] [:db/cas 17592186311740 :question/problem "Compute the exact value of the definite integral.\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{1}{4x-1}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "Compute the exact value of the definite integral.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{1}{4x-1}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186311741 :question/problem "Compute the exact value of the definite integral.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{10{x}^{3}}{5{x}^{4}+4}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "Compute the exact value of the definite integral.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{10{x}^{3}}{5{x}^{4}+4}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186311745 :question/problem "Compute the exact value of the definite integral.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{6x}{\\sqrt{{({x}^{2}+1)}^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "Compute the exact value of the definite integral.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{6x}{\\sqrt{{({x}^{2}+1)}^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186311746 :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $10\\,Ω,$ and an inductor of inductance $2\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RL circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=10\\,Ω.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=10I\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=2\\,\\text{H}.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=2\\cdot \\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ in the circuit is\n\n$$\n6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)=10I+2\\cdot \\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}+5I=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${I}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{I}_{c}}{dt}+5{I}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-5t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $2t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $2t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t), \\\\ \\frac{d{I}_{p}}{dt} & =-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))+5(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ (-2\\alpha +5\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+(5\\alpha +2\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-2\\alpha +5\\beta &=3\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) \\\\ 5\\alpha +2\\beta &=0\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{6}{29},$ $\\beta =\\frac{15}{29}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{6}{29}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{15}{29}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)={I}_{c}(t)+{I}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-5t}-\\frac{6}{29}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{15}{29}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-5t}$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The function ${I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{6}{29}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{15}{29}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)$ is the *steady-state current*. It governs the current's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nI(t)≈{I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{6}{29}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{15}{29}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state current is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{\\left(-\\frac{6}{29}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{15}{29}\\right)}^{2}}≈0.56\\,\\text{A},\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." "The diagram shows a series RL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $10\\,Ω,$ and an inductor of inductance $2\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RL circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=10\\,Ω.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=10I\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=2\\,\\text{H}.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=2\\cdot \\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ in the circuit is\n\n$$\n6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)=10I+2\\cdot \\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}+5I=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${I}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{I}_{c}}{dt}+5{I}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-5t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $2t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $2t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t), \\\\ \\frac{d{I}_{p}}{dt} & =-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))+5(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ (-2\\alpha +5\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+(5\\alpha +2\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-2\\alpha +5\\beta &=3\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) \\\\ 5\\alpha +2\\beta &=0\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{6}{29},$ $\\beta =\\frac{15}{29}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{6}{29}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{15}{29}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)={I}_{c}(t)+{I}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-5t}-\\frac{6}{29}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{15}{29}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-5t}$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The function $$\n\\displaystyle {I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{6}{29}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{15}{29}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\n$$ is the *steady-state current*. It governs the current's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nI(t)≈{I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{6}{29}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{15}{29}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state current is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{\\left(-\\frac{6}{29}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{15}{29}\\right)}^{2}}≈0.56\\,\\text{A},\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186311747 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})}=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186311749 :questi