general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-4t}-\\frac{10}{41}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+\\frac{8}{41}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-4t}$ is the *transient charge*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The function $$\n\\displaystyle {q}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{10}{41}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+\\frac{8}{41}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\n$$ is the *steady-state charge*. It governs the charge's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nq(t)≈{q}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{10}{41}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+\\frac{8}{41}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state charge, in coulombs, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{\\left(-\\frac{10}{41}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{8}{41}\\right)}^{2}}≈0.31,\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186311843 :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RC circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $75\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor with resistance $25\\,Ω,$ and a capacitor with capacitance $\\frac{1}{200}\\,\\text{F}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs. \n\n$$\nI=\\frac{\\text{d}q}{\\text{d}t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RC circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=25\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=25I=25\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{\\left(\\frac{1}{200}\\right)}=200q.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n75\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)=25\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+200q,\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}+8q=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t).\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+8{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-8t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $6t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $6t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t), \\\\ \\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt} & =-6\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)+6\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-6\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)+6\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t))+8(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)) & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t) \\\\ (-6\\alpha +8\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)+(8\\alpha +6\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t) & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-6\\alpha +8\\beta &=0\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)) \\\\ 8\\alpha +6\\beta &=3\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t))\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =\\frac{6}{25},\\beta =\\frac{9}{50}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\frac{6}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)+\\frac{9}{50}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-8t}+\\frac{6}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)+\\frac{9}{50}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-8t}$ is the *transient charge*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The function ${q}_{p}(t)=\\frac{6}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)+\\frac{9}{50}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)$ is the *steady-state charge*. It governs the charge's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nq(t)≈{q}_{p}(t)=\\frac{6}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)+\\frac{9}{50}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state charge, in coulombs, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{6}{25}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{9}{50}\\right)}^{2}}=0.3.\n$$" "The diagram shows a series RC circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $75\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor with resistance $25\\,Ω,$ and a capacitor with capacitance $\\frac{1}{200}\\,\\text{F}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs. \n\n$$\nI=\\frac{\\text{d}q}{\\text{d}t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RC circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=25\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=25I=25\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{\\left(\\frac{1}{200}\\right)}=200q.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge 