ing the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-3\\alpha +4\\beta &=2\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) \\\\ 4\\alpha +3\\beta &=0\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t))\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $$\n\\displaystyle \\alpha =-\\frac{6}{25},\\beta =\\frac{8}{25}.\n$$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{6}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{8}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-4t}-\\frac{6}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{8}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-4t}$ is the *transient charge*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The function $$\n\\displaystyle {q}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{6}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{8}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\n$$ is the *steady-state charge*. It governs the charge's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nq(t)≈{q}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{6}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{8}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state charge, in coulombs, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{\\left(-\\frac{6}{25}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{8}{25}\\right)}^{2}}=0.4.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186311845 :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RC circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $40\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor with resistance $20\\,Ω,$ and a capacitor with capacitance $\\frac{1}{200}\\,\\text{F}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs. \n\n$$\nI=\\frac{\\text{d}q}{\\text{d}t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RC circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=20\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=20I=20\\,\\frac{dq}{dt},\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{\\left(\\frac{1}{200}\\right)}=200q.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n40\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)=20\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+200q,\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}+10q=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+10{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-10t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $2t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $2t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t), \\\\ \\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt} & =-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))+10(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ (-2\\alpha +10\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+(10\\alpha +2\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-2\\alpha +10\\beta &=0\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) \\\\ 10\\alpha +2\\beta &=2\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =\\frac{5}{26},\\beta =\\frac{1}{26}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\frac{5}{26}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{1}{26}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-10t}+\\frac{5}{26}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{1}{26}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-10t}$ is the *transient charge*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The function ${q}_{p}(t)=\\frac{5}{26}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{1}{26}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)$ is the *steady-state charge*. It governs the charge's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nq(t)≈{q}_{p}(t)=\\frac{5}{26}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{1}{26}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state charge, in coulombs, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{5}{26}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{1}{26}\\right)}^{2}}≈0.20,\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." "The diagram shows a series RC circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $40\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor with resistance $20\\,Ω,$ and a capacitor with capacitance $\\frac{1}{200}\\,\\text{F}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs. \n\n$$\nI=\\frac{\\text{d}q}{\\text{d}t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RC circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=20\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=20I=20\\,\\frac{dq}{dt},\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{\\left(\\frac{1}{200}\\right)}=200q.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n40\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)=20\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+200q,\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}+10q=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+10{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-10t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $2t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $2t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t), \\\\ \\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt} & =-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))+10(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ (-2\\alpha +10\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+(10\\alpha +2\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-2\\alpha +10\\beta &=0\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) \\\\ 10\\alpha +2\\beta &=2\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $$\n\\displaystyle \\alpha =\\frac{5}{26},\\beta =\\frac{1}{26}.\n$$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\frac{5}{26}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{1}{26}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-10t}+\\frac{5}{26}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{1}{26}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-10t}$ is the *transient charge*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The function $$\n\\displaystyle {q}_{p}(t)=\\frac{5}{26}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{1}{26}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\n$$ is the *steady-state charge*. It governs the charge's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nq(t)≈{q}_{p}(t)=\\frac{5}{26}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{1}{26}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state charge, in coulombs, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{5}{26}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{1}{26}\\right)}^{2}}≈0.20,\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186311846 :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RC circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $80\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor with resistance $20\\,Ω,$ and a capacitor with capacitance $\\frac{1}{100}\\,\\text{F}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs. \n\n$$\nI=\\frac{\\text{d}q}{\\text{d}t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RC circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=20\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=20I=20\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{\\left(\\frac{1}{100}\\right)}=100q.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n80\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)=20\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+100q,\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}+5q=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+5{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-5t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $4t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $4t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t), \\\\ \\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt} & =-4\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+4\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-4\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+4\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t))+5(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)) & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ (-4\\alpha +5\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+(5\\alpha +4\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-4\\alpha +5\\beta &=4\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)) \\\\ 5\\alpha +4\\beta &=0\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t))\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{16}{41},\\beta =\\frac{20}{41}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{16}{41}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\frac{20}{41}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-5t}-\\frac{16}{41}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\frac{20}{41}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-5t}$ is the *transient charge*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The function ${q}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{16}{41}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\frac{20}{41}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$ is the *steady-state charge*. It governs the charge's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nq(t)≈{q}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{16}{41}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\frac{20}{41}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state charge, in coulombs, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{\\left(-\\frac{16}{41}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{20}{41}\\right)}^{2}}≈0.62,\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." "The diagram shows a series RC circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $80\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor with resistance $20\\,Ω,$ and a capacitor with capacitance $\\frac{1}{100}\\,\\text{F}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs. \n\n$$\nI=\\frac{\\text{d}q}{\\text{d}t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RC circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=20\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=20I=20\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{\\left(\\frac{1}{100}\\right)}=100q.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n80\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)=20\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+100q,\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}+5q=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+5{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-5t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $4t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $4t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t), \\\\ \\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt} & =-4\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+4\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-4\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+4\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t))+5(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)) & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ (-4\\alpha +5\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+(5\\alpha +4\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-4\\alpha +5\\beta &=4\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)) \\\\ 5\\alpha +4\\beta &=0\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t))\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $$\n\\displaystyle \\alpha =-\\frac{16}{41},\\beta =\\frac{20}{41}.\n$$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{16}{41}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\frac{20}{41}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-5t}-\\frac{16}{41}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\frac{20}{41}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-5t}$ is the *transient charge*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The function $$\n\\displaystyle {q}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{16}{41}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\frac{20}{41}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\n$$ is the *steady-state charge*. It governs the charge's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nq(t)≈{q}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{16}{41}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\frac{20}{41}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state charge, in coulombs, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{\\left(-\\frac{16}{41}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{20}{41}\\right)}^{2}}≈0.62,\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186311865 :question/problem "Calculate the product $Q{I}_{3}$ given that ${I}_{3}$ is the $3\\times 3$ identity matrix and\n$Q=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $Q\\cdot {I}_{3}=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-4}} | {{field-5}} | {{field-6}} |  |\n|  |  | {{field-7}} | {{field-8}} | {{field-9}} |  |" "Calculate the product $Q{I}_{3}$ given that ${I}_{3}$ is the $3\\times 3$ identity matrix and\n$$\n\\displaystyle Q=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $Q\\cdot {I}_{3}=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-4}} | {{field-5}} | {{field-6}} |  |\n|  |  | {{field-7}} | {{field-8}} | {{field-9}} |  |"] [:db/cas 17592186311867 :question/problem "Calculate the product ${I}_{3}B$ given that ${I}_{3}$ is the $3\\times 3$ identity matrix and\n$B=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -6 \\\\ -5 & 7 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${I}_{3}\\cdot B=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-4}} | {{field-5}} | {{field-6}} |  |\n|  |  | {{field-7}} | {{field-8}} | {{field-9}} |  |" "Calculate the product ${I}_{3}B$ given that ${I}_{3}$ is the $3\\times 3$ identity matrix and\n$$\n\\displaystyle B=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -6 \\\\ -5 & 7 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${I}_{3}\\cdot B=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-4}} | {{field-5}} | {{field-6}} |  |\n|  |  | 